WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.181 --> 00:00:09.490
- I wanna welcome everybody.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.730
My name is Paul North.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.660
I'm a professor in the
German Department at Yale

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.380
and this is the first
in a series of workshops

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.800
that carries the title "The
Value of Marx's Capital."

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.520
Obviously a little play on words there.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.870
I wanna start each of these episodes

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:30.260
with a quote from the author
who has been kind enough

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.330
to come to our screens.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.030
And this is a quote from Terrell
Carver who's with us today.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.487
It goes like this, quote,

00:00:37.487 --> 00:00:40.140
"No one who wants to
read a work of history,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.060
even a history of capitalism,

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.590
will find this book easy to understand."

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.047
He's referring to Marx's "Capital."

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.367
"But then," he adds,

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:51.840
"capitalism is not an
easy phenomenon to grasp

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.547
or an easy one to live through."

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.530
I want to recognize a
whole bunch of people

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.420
as we're starting.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.900
First, the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.100
Alice Kaplan,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.280
for being kind enough to
let us try this experiment

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.230
in the middle of a pandemic.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:14.930
I want to thank Sandra Malan-Bowles

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.200
for her incredible organizational skills,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.870
and Leana Hirschfeld-Kroen
who is the graduate student

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.160
that has pulled this all together.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.220
I want to thank also Yasmin

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.083
who is going to handle
the technical parts here.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.440
This is a set of workshops that
run in tandem with a seminar

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.150
in which there are about 25 undergraduates

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.083
who are reading through Marx's
"Capital" very carefully.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.370
So I want to welcome the
students in the seminar.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:52.630
And then I'd like to
thank the Franke family

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.670
for making possible these lectures,

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.390
the Franke Lectures in the Humanities,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.360
that is Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.780
who gave this money to
present important topics

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.683
in the humanities to a
wide and general audience.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.640
One of the reasons we're
doing this is because

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.640
I am involved in a project to edit

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.570
a new translation into English

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.710
of Marx's "Capital, Volume I"

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.630
and I have to thank my
collaborator for that,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:25.160
Paul Reitter of OSU whom I
want to introduce to you now.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.590
I'm not sure you'll be able to see him

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:31.240
but he will come in later
when it's question time.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.040
Paul Reitter is a translator of some note,

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.740
also a scholar of German studies,

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.080
and he is rendering "Capital,
Volume I" into an English

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.860
that you can understand

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.470
and that will also delight you.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.770
This volume should come out
with Princeton University Press

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.053
in 2022 we hope.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.780
Now it's a great pleasure
to introduce Terrell Carver,

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.700
who is a professor of Political Theory

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.800
in the School of Sociology, Politics,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.360
and International Studies

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.633
at the University of Bristol in the UK.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.390
Terrell Carver has been a visiting fellow

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.380
at the Research School of Social Sciences

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.680
of the Australian National University,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.730
at the Center for Asian
and Pacific Studies

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.110
at Seikei University in Tokyo.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.100
He has been visiting
professor in the States

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:24.100
and many other times in
Australia, in the Far East.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.450
Most notable for our interests
is that he was a member of

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.250
the Editorial Commission on
the German Marx-Engels Edition,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.490
the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe,

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:38.980
otherwise known as the MEGA,

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.950
which is the most significant event

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.390
in the textual fund of Marxian writings

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.423
ever it seems to me.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.910
Terrell Carver specializes
in Marx, Engels, and Marxism

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.830
and on the philosophy and
methodology of social science.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:01.180
He has written widely on political theory

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.410
but has a big bookshelf
on Marx and Engels.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.450
Specifically, he wrote the book on Marx

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.260
or a book on Marx, that is "Marx"

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.890
in Polity Press from 2018.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.790
There are many books on Marx

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.190
but there are very few books on Engels

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.040
and Terrell Carver, I think
you would have to admit

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.280
is the world expert on Engels.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.530
He wrote the book many years ago now

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.227
but it's still a very important book,

00:04:28.227 --> 00:04:31.130
"Marx and Engels: The
Intellectual Relationship."

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.720
There is no Marx without Engels

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.343
as I'm sure Terrell would agree.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:37.283
This book is from 1984.

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:41.430
He wrote "The Life and
Thought of Friedrich Engels"

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.160
which came out in 1990

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.640
and was reissued this year,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.000
and was a co-editor on
"Engels Before Marx"

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.860
which is a very important
book to understand

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.023
where Engels came from.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:57.790
I'd like to give the microphone
over to Professor Carver

00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.680
but first I want to thank him for a book

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.130
that was important for me.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:04.530
That's "The Postmodern Marx"
which came out in 1998.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.930
That's the first book that made me think

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.260
I wanted to read "Marx."

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.213
So thank you and welcome.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.360
- Well, I'm delighted
and honored to be here

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.880
and really pleased to
hear that comment about

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.480
that particular book.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.200
I wish you'd been a reviewer

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.500
but we'll get on with the
plot here this evening.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.190
I'm also delighted to
hear that it's a workshop,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.607
so I expect to work out

00:05:35.607 --> 00:05:38.000
and I expect the audience to work too.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.653
I think there are lots of you

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.780
and I'm sorry, I can't see everybody

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.300
and interact in that way,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.270
but we'll do what we can with this medium.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:54.210
Thanks, particularly to Leana and Yasmin

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.870
for helping me along through some of this,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:02.870
and I'm still tackling the
Yale forms as we speak.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.653
But everybody's very patient.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:10.090
Just a little bit of background.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:13.510
I've been in the UK for quite a long time

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:16.240
but I am actually originally American.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.080
So I'm just mentioning that

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.080
so that we don't feel the need
for a lot of intercultural

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.880
translation exercises.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.730
I actually graduated
from Columbia in 1968.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:34.180
I went to high school
with a flag out the front

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.210
and took the SATs,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.330
and did all of that.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:43.330
So, I'm not a visitor who
sort of dropped in from Mars

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:47.530
although Paul North did
say that with this on

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.640
I look like a helicopter pilot.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:54.640
So, I thought we'd shut up and get going

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.200
with a few slides just
as a kind of warm up.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.290
So I'll see if I can share the screen here

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:06.060
and set up PowerPoint

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:10.600
which just looks like it's
actually working right now.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.707
So I've called this talk

00:07:13.707 --> 00:07:18.707
"Das Kapital Critique,
History, and Knowledge"

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.920
and I'll explain why I've called it that.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.740
And this is a bit of framing exercise

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:31.090
that sets up philosophy and methodology

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:36.090
so that what I say about
capital, the text and about Marx

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.390
makes some kind of sense.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.550
Perhaps as a bit of a
health warning in the fit,

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.240
you probably won't get
this kind of outlook

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.710
or this kind of approach
from the other speakers

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.893
in the series, most of whom I know,

00:07:53.316 --> 00:07:55.293
and some are quite close colleagues,

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:00.260
but I hope it's refreshing.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.260
And there's a bit of a
methodological angle,

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.190
I'm kind of inclined
so Paul North will know

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.200
from "The Postmodern Marx"

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.530
to take an idea and
put it out very clearly

00:08:12.918 --> 00:08:15.090
of what some people
consider to be an extreme

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.980
and unsettling form

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.467
of just to see how much
mileage there is in the idea

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:23.870
and what kind of pushback there is on it.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.560
So, there should be some
lively debate this evening.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.543
So if I can change the slide.

00:08:32.787 --> 00:08:33.620
There we are.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.990
I think it's quite important
in these exercises,

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:39.690
whether literary or political theory,

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:44.310
to get visually acquainted with who it is

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.710
that we're making speak to us

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:52.360
or imagining talking
to us through the text.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.370
So, here we have two images of Marx.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:09:00.250
These are actually the first
images after the early 1840s,

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.940
the early photographs.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.337
The one on the left 1861

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:09.890
and the one on the right 1867.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:10.970
Just the year that

00:09:13.417 --> 00:09:17.120
"Capital" is published for the first time.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.443
So changing the slide.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.220
This is the way I like
to think about Marx.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:27.690
This is him in his university uniform

00:09:28.687 --> 00:09:30.700
as a Prussian student.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.943
He's about 22.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:37.840
And he's just on the term
of leaving university,

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:41.363
getting his PhD by post
which is a great idea.

00:09:42.811 --> 00:09:46.330
And leaving academia forever.

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:48.890
And it's quite important to understand

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:53.520
how repressive, reactionary,
neo-feudal, no nothing

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:59.560
the German kingdom states, principalities

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:03.860
disunited as they were into 40 odd units,

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.900
plus the Austrian empire.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:10.320
How reactionary unmodern this was

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.620
in terms of being repressive

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:18.450
and this apply to the university.

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:23.450
So his main tutor was chased
out of the university.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:27.900
Karl knew that he was never
gonna get a university job

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:31.050
and he gave up on that pretty quickly.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:35.140
So, the book on the left is the sort of

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:37.700
volcanic one of 2018

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.857
that Paul North was talking about.

00:10:40.857 --> 00:10:45.257
And the first chapter is
called "Making Marx Marx."

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.260
There are two things going on here,

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.527
it's a kind of double (indistinct) really.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.950
That is we have him here as a student

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.280
about to take a great turn in his life,

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:02.763
embark on making himself himself.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.800
But the himself as he made himself

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:11.370
isn't really the same guy that he became

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.100
in what we call his reception

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.120
or how he was constructed and written up

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:23.130
for the history books and for the record.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:27.220
So that latter story starts
in the very early 1870s

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.680
and it's quite difficult
to explain to people now

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.530
how obscure he was, how unfamous he was,

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:38.700
how there were lots of other guys around

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:42.830
who didn't appear to
be that much different.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:47.830
Most of the memoir of this we
have date from 40 years later.

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:52.330
So people constructed
an idea after the fact

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:53.773
about what he was like.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.430
And then we can project
that back into the letters

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.720
and other things that we have
are actually contemporary

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.390
with this process.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:07.390
But methodologically I'm quite
keen to unpick and unpack

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:09.800
that kind of enterprise.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.800
So in the early 1870s he
was constructed as a guru

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:19.610
and in a sense, a kind of founding father

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.360
of German socialism.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.630
Right at the moment just
after the introduction

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:28.160
of the imperial constitution in 1871

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.533
when really for the first time,

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:34.610
at least a few people
could vote for something

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:36.580
that might make some difference,

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.630
although in practice,
probably not very much.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.900
Again, it's a battle historically

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:46.280
to try to explain to people
how deeply undemocratic.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:49.890
Even the constitutional
regimes of the times

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:54.010
actually where universal manhood suffrage

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:57.960
didn't hit the United Kingdom
'til the 1880s for instance,

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:02.877
and here we are only in the
early 1870s in the Reich.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.463
So, at this point,

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:13.250
the two branches of the socialist movement

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:15.610
are getting together to unite.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:19.350
One had been led by Ferdinand Lassalle.

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:21.530
He was rather conveniently dead

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.940
and it's quite easy to honor
people when they're dead

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:26.840
and they don't get in the way anymore.

00:13:27.686 --> 00:13:32.686
And those who had Marx as
a mentor by correspondence

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.020
'cause of course he was living in London,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:39.260
and he visited him from time to time

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.050
and were younger.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.010
Thought that their
grouping within socialism,

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:49.010
their proto-political party
should have honorary founders

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:54.300
to rival the Ferdinand Lassalle
who was really quite famous

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:57.560
in the German states at the time

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.140
which Marx was not.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:03.250
He had a certain notoriety as a 48er

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:07.163
who'd gone into exile because
he was prosecuted for treason.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.540
But that was about it.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.490
So at that point, Marx and Engels

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.713
were created and constructed
as important thinkers.

00:14:17.205 --> 00:14:19.163
And at the time that was done,

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:23.560
not with "Das Kapital"
which was recently published

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:28.560
but with "The Communist
Manifesto" of 1848.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:31.580
Marx and Engels were quite puzzled by this

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.620
and a bit standoffish.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:38.620
And so, sort of wrote saying
that thing is 25 years old.

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.180
Nobody's read it since,

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.530
we've really pretty much forgotten it.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:48.110
It does get one footnote in
"Das Kapital" interestingly,

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:53.110
but nobody in 1867 could
have got hold of a copy.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:58.060
Most of the copies have been
seized by the Prussian police.

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:00.900
It was really a rare pamphlet.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:03.930
So they said, why on earth
don't you write your own?

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:06.650
Which is one for now,

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.840
which is quite a reasonable
political question.

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:11.560
However, they were persuaded
to go along with this

00:15:12.513 --> 00:15:15.670
process of constructing icons

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:20.510
and being made into symbols,

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:23.880
and gurus who would have works

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:28.880
which were then republished
through the later 1870s, 1880s,

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:35.950
and this is part of the
political story of socialism

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:37.823
as an intellectual movement.

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:43.780
So if we go right back to the beginning,

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:49.860
what actually happens with young Karl here

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.890
as he's about to leave university

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:56.110
and can't get an academic job?

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.363
Where exactly does he go?

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:04.650
He goes to a newspaper
office that is in Cologne,

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.693
not that far up the river.

00:16:08.946 --> 00:16:13.946
A group of businessmen and
some French intellectuals

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:19.110
of the sort who are dedicated
to popular sovereignty,

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:23.140
liberalism, tinges of socialism

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:25.010
derived from French thinking.

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:26.760
There are little collective who want

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.010
to gather newspaper together.

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:31.620
This is actually quite difficult

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:34.320
and he becomes an associates,

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:39.320
and he tells us in his own
autobiographical sketch of 1859

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:45.330
that it's in this office
where he first made contact

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:50.400
with what he calls, quote,
"material interests," unquote.

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.760
Well, this is a well-known
biographical fact

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:57.040
but I've chosen in chapter one
in this book to pause on that

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.730
and try to think it
through quite carefully.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:03.000
So, here we've got a university student

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:07.220
from a professional
intellectual background

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:10.293
in Trier, provincial backwater.

00:17:11.268 --> 00:17:15.900
Comes to Cologne where
some enterprising types

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:20.900
are dedicating themselves to
politics, trade, and commerce.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.940
This is quite a big
jump for an intellectual

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:29.410
and Marx clearly found
it really stimulating.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:32.110
In other words, from his
education and philosophy,

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:33.800
history and jurisprudence,

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:38.000
he had learned really nothing
about trade, commerce,

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.980
and what industry might
be like given that in

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.490
the German states there
really was hardly any,

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:51.490
and then tucked away in
rather nasty little pockets.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.720
So in this office,

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.410
we don't know very much
about what happened.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:01.010
So not very much of this gets written

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:03.610
into the historical record.

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:06.230
We know who the businessmen were,

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.840
who were the backers of the paper

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:12.050
and who were, would be editors.

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:15.520
We know whom they wanted to
be editor which wasn't Marx,

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:18.280
it was actually Friedrich List

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:23.280
the becoming famous politically economist

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:28.510
in the German-speaking land.

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:33.510
So this was quite a new
kind of scientific subject

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.710
and it's important to
understand that in this kind of

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:39.130
neo-feudal reactionary order

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:42.430
making money through trade and
commerce was not very nice.

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:46.670
It wasn't something that
realists really very much wanted.

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:48.540
They wanted peasants with taxes

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:53.540
and so, have an easy
life and be landowners,

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:58.160
and not have modernizing change.

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:02.280
So within this reactionary regime

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:04.410
the press is strictly controlled as is

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:07.550
even conversation on subjects

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:09.640
that are taken to be political

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:12.000
which certainly includes anything to do

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.413
with religious doubt.

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:18.250
And the police are
around snooping on people

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:19.760
and giving them warnings

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.580
and all that kind of thing.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:25.610
And of course, that had happened
to Marx's father in Trier.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:29.430
So, these are not
inconsiderable kinds of things

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:34.430
to reckon with when you're
entering a newspaper office

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:37.080
and getting involved with people

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:40.763
who do believe in
liberalization to a point.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:44.053
Believing in popular
sovereignty doesn't mean

00:19:44.053 --> 00:19:47.230
that you believed even in
universal manhood suffrage,

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:49.100
far from it.

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:51.900
Most people who believed in

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:53.970
some version of popular sovereignty

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:58.220
certainly didn't think that
uneducated people should vote.

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:01.510
So the story of democracy
is not really about

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:04.420
the proclamation of votes for everybody.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:07.590
The story of democracy
is really much more about

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:11.840
whom can we continue to exclude

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:16.350
whilst including a few more
than were there before.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:19.060
So that's a bit of background

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:22.590
that this chapter actually sets off

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:27.230
and I think that should do
us a bit of an introduction

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:32.230
to how I conceive of Marx an author

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:37.050
and in particular as a political actor.

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:39.570
And so, I'll come on to that.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.800
First of all, let's go
for some more visuals.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:46.340
Here we have "Das
Kapital," the book itself.

00:20:46.340 --> 00:20:48.320
Here's the first edition.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:51.010
Again, I think it's quite
important in these things

00:20:51.010 --> 00:20:54.900
to get some sense of
physicality in the original

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:58.920
so it is not a kind of disembodied text

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:02.620
that just exists in a logo world

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:06.290
as meanings and concepts.

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:08.970
It's actually a book written in German

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:11.960
for German-speaking audience.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:13.973
That's what it looked like.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:19.820
We know from the first
chapter where Marx mentions

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:24.820
his earlier so-called half
volume or first installment,

00:21:25.297 --> 00:21:29.080
"Zur Kritik der Politischen Okonomie."

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:33.943
And that slat edition, that
little half volume of 1859.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.413
Some of my Germanist friends
can help me out here.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:43.130
I'm interested in their transition

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:47.110
from the Gothic typeface
to the Roman typeface.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.390
I mean, they were both around,

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.480
I just wonder if anybody
thinks there's any particular

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:55.060
significance whether in
the place of publication

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:57.250
or the target audience.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:58.480
I don't know.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:00.530
Gothic typeface of course was around 'til

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:03.380
well into the 20th century

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:06.140
and my German course at Columbia,

00:22:06.140 --> 00:22:07.740
we all learned to read that,

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:10.080
and I'm not sure that

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:13.430
undergraduates necessarily
do that anymore.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:18.430
So here's a couple of things
for us to look at together

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:23.190
so in the mood and to
looking the right direction.

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:26.840
Part of this is to remind Anglophones

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:28.860
like ourselves in this gathering

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.620
that this is a German,

00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:36.810
writing in German for a
German political audience.

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:41.810
Marx's political world
is really looking east.

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:47.150
He looks westward to France for culture,

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:49.770
for socialism, for communism,

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:52.040
for intellectual stimulation,

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:56.290
and for political economy
in the first instance.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.320
So educated in the Rhineland

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:01.930
he was completely fluent in
French from an early age.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.987
And if you're seeing the recent biopic

00:23:04.987 --> 00:23:08.187
"Le jeune Marx" or "The Younger Marx,"

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:11.470
that's something they actually got right

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:16.240
in this German-Belgian co-production.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:20.710
The characters in it
speak quite indifferently

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:22.650
to each other in German and French

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:26.353
as they go along with suitable subtitling.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:32.820
So, we know that in learning French,

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:39.240
he also learned Roman style handwriting.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:42.590
I did actually discover or track down

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:47.163
some previously unpublished
letters of his in London.

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:50.930
And when these kind of
fell out of a plastic bag

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:52.933
on someone's kitchen table,

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:57.700
I realized that I could
read his handwriting

00:23:57.700 --> 00:23:59.900
quite easily on some.

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:00.790
And so I thought,

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:02.350
well, that's 'cause
he's writing in English,

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:05.760
but actually he learned
how to write cursive Roman

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:08.690
when as a school boy he learned French.

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:10.920
Otherwise he have the famously terrible

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.890
hand handwriting in German cursive Gothic

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:17.100
which is almost impossible to decipher

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:20.030
although, I mean, perhaps
some of my colleagues here

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:24.860
spend their time on
manuscripts from that era.

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:27.770
So that's a little bit
more of the background.

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:32.770
I'm fighting against the
idea in the Anglophone world

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:38.150
that having remove to
London at 1849, 1850,

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.330
and living there the rest of their lives

00:24:41.330 --> 00:24:43.117
into the 1880s and 1890s,

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:48.230
Marx and Engels became honorary Englishmen

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:50.930
and speak to us in English.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:55.020
And we now have 50 volumes of Marx

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:58.900
translated into English.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:01.840
Engels is a completely
different case of course.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:03.747
His English was very fluent

00:25:03.747 --> 00:25:07.893
and he wrote and published
in it from a very early age,

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:11.573
barely 20, 21 years old,

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:16.163
because his dad took him
on business to England.

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:21.440
Marx's English was much more rudimentary

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:22.943
learned rather slowly.

00:25:23.922 --> 00:25:26.380
He only got on with
reading English authors

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:31.319
as the 1840s progressed, 1845, six.

00:25:31.319 --> 00:25:34.710
And his first visit to
England is an 1845 with Engels

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:37.393
to go to the library in Manchester.

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:44.060
His English was workable

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:49.060
and so he, for money, got
to write it quite fluently

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:52.000
in the 1850s and early 1860s.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:54.050
But I think it's important to remember

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:55.040
that even in London,

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.390
his politics is pretty much lifting East

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:01.560
and so looking West to the US East Coast,

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:05.667
and so the Midwest to the German
speaking communities there,

00:26:05.667 --> 00:26:08.220
and some of you will know
that some of his works

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:10.850
were actually published there

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:13.630
within that community.

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:18.340
So, a little bit of
philosophical backgrounds

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:22.550
just to set things up.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:24.420
I've put critique down here

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:30.290
to say that this is not
just a method or a genre,

00:26:30.290 --> 00:26:31.123
there it's that,

00:26:31.123 --> 00:26:35.830
but for Marx it's actually an
ontology and an epistemology.

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:41.300
So this is where things get a bit

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:44.503
radical or extreme or,

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:46.940
I mean, Paul North will know this

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:48.770
from "The Postmodern Marx."

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:51.270
That is, it's an ontology and epistemology

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:53.440
that is what there is to know

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:56.633
and how we know it of
political intervention.

00:26:57.570 --> 00:26:59.830
In communicative practice

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:04.520
and the communicative
practice is meaning-making

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:09.520
as a repetitious citational activity.

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:14.130
So that perhaps doesn't
mean a lot on its own

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:16.670
but as the discussion goes on,

00:27:16.670 --> 00:27:20.290
I think it will become
a little bit clearer.

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:24.370
I put everything, I put history down to

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:30.150
put a few more things on the board.

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:32.790
That is to say within this ontology,

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:34.860
epistemology of meaning-making,

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:36.613
everything is historical.

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:40.810
Human history making is what there is

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:43.940
and historiography is what we say

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:47.980
about human history making
that we want to say.

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:51.090
And of course a lot is excluded

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:56.090
as we will know from discussions
about historiography.

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.530
And so, inclusion and exclusion from

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:03.210
about the last 40 years.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.750
This is because everything
is communicative

00:28:06.750 --> 00:28:09.333
if we want it to be communicative.

00:28:11.533 --> 00:28:13.450
That is if we're making meanings,

00:28:13.450 --> 00:28:15.600
we project those meanings outwards,

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:18.550
and then we read them
off as what the thing is

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:21.100
or what it is telling us.

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:24.070
Existence in a philosophical sense

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:28.160
is therefore meaningful
only within meaning.

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:31.070
It's not something outside of meaning

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:34.500
that is a validation point for meaning.

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:37.110
So some people will recognize this

00:28:38.119 --> 00:28:41.023
and it may seem a bit heavy for an intro,

00:28:41.930 --> 00:28:45.540
but I hope it'll pay off or
at least give you the idea

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:48.747
of how this is set up

00:28:48.747 --> 00:28:51.720
and not perhaps what you ordinarily hear.

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.250
So within this,

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:56.633
knowledge is always and already purposive.

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:59.703
It's not abstract.

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:04.600
Nor is it unconnected
with political projects.

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:07.560
So one feature of capital of course

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:10.490
is to argue that political economy,

00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:12.990
the economics of the time is not

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:16.340
a disinterested science done by scientists

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:20.903
who stand outside society
and outside politics.

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:25.410
It is rather itself a political project

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:29.833
and it says some things and
obfuscates or hides others.

00:29:31.314 --> 00:29:33.880
So that's all entirely fair enough

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:38.013
and to be expected from
this point of view.

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:42.940
Science, therefore,
from Marxian perspective

00:29:42.940 --> 00:29:45.790
and you can see this
in very early writings,

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:47.950
is technology,

00:29:47.950 --> 00:29:49.650
is industry,

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:51.300
is commerce,

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:53.380
is capitalism.

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:57.700
So there's no kind of disinterested
zone of pure knowledge

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:00.370
and ask anybody who knows any scientist

00:30:00.370 --> 00:30:01.833
where their money comes from.

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:07.020
And so, you can see that this is

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:09.603
an ongoing idea.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:17.340
The working through of this perspective

00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:23.060
in terms of Marx and his writings

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:25.700
and how they can be approached,

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:27.880
and I'm not saying this is the only way,

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:30.527
but just that it might be an
interesting and productive way,

00:30:30.527 --> 00:30:33.900
and it does make a bit of
a change for most people.

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:36.030
This philosophical perspective

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:41.860
is worked out in a chapter in this book

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:43.383
which is published.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:47.967
It's chapter 13 and the title is there,

00:30:47.967 --> 00:30:51.750
"Socializing Knowledge
and Historicizing Society:

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:56.530
Marx and Engels and The
Manuscripts of 1845-46."

00:30:56.530 --> 00:31:01.530
So, just to geek out on this,

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:07.510
the argument is put through
from the roughest pages

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:10.940
of the manuscripts that have gone into the

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:14.240
so-called German ideology,

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.973
it's the so-called book with
a so-called forever chapter.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:21.380
This argument is actually fleshed out

00:31:21.380 --> 00:31:23.100
from these very, very rough pages

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:26.440
which really are unique in the legacy

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:29.500
because they're the only
manuscript pages we've got

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:31.270
where Marx and Engels are actually

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:33.500
drafting things together.

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:35.340
And the only pages that we've got

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:37.700
where they're making extensive

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.520
rough draft corrections, crossing out,

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:44.060
changing one word literally into another,

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.763
and rephrasing things as they go along.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:49.470
In those particular pages

00:31:49.470 --> 00:31:50.980
which actually they excluded

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:52.950
from the things they were drafting,

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:57.050
I think probably on grounds
they were too theoretical.

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:58.877
These pages are interesting to us

00:31:58.877 --> 00:32:01.780
and the object here is not to turn Marx

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:05.180
into a philosopher or an economist,

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:09.960
but rather to see what an interesting

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:14.250
view of society, knowledge, and history

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:18.353
arises through this method of critique.

00:32:19.672 --> 00:32:23.993
And therefore, I think
you can see a vocabulary

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.890
kind of moving from one word, one phrase,

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:31.190
from one phrasing to another,

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:34.640
as that argument develops.

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:39.640
So, I'll leave the slides
at this particular point

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:45.850
and do a little bit of reading.

00:32:45.850 --> 00:32:47.530
I did some pre-circulation

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:49.190
of something that I've written on capital

00:32:49.190 --> 00:32:52.170
that's actually a 10,000 words.

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:54.330
It's still in draft.

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:59.330
It's going into something
that's written for historians.

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:03.357
It's called, Bloomsbury the publisher,

00:33:03.357 --> 00:33:05.457
"History, Theory, and Method."

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:09.650
And it's written through
a kind of template.

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:13.160
So I'll read a few excerpts from that

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:16.260
to pick out some quotes
from this amazing book

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:18.710
so that we actually get
to hear some of Marx here

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:24.033
and not just to hear me.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:30.020
But I thought it was quite unusual

00:33:30.020 --> 00:33:31.650
and sort of puzzling choice

00:33:33.410 --> 00:33:38.240
in saying this reference book chapter

00:33:38.240 --> 00:33:40.480
is going to be for historians,

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:45.480
and is going to be about
Marx then choosing capital.

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:50.620
Because it's not considered
a work of history,

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:55.620
it doesn't fit historiography as a genre.

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:03.320
Marx isn't really rated as a
historian by historians mostly.

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:07.600
They've generally picked him apart

00:34:07.600 --> 00:34:10.760
where they have taken the view
that he was doing history,

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:12.930
but doing it badly.

00:34:12.930 --> 00:34:15.510
As I would say, he never
had an academic job,

00:34:15.510 --> 00:34:19.810
didn't want to be an academic
historian or philosopher,

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:20.710
or an economist

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:25.270
so I was quite puzzled
that they chose capital

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:28.480
and also quite excited.

00:34:28.480 --> 00:34:31.300
But it is quite a stretch for historians

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:33.700
as it is indeed for anybody

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:38.400
to pick up this book
and start on page one.

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:40.930
So quite a lot of this 10,000 words

00:34:40.930 --> 00:34:44.800
is devoted to unraveling

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:50.650
the kind of puzzle that Paul
North gave me the intro.

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:55.400
That is how on earth do we
start reading this book?

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:56.910
And what do we make of it?

00:34:56.910 --> 00:34:59.890
Well, this kind of takes
me back to the political

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:02.250
context of the time.

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:05.840
Virtually everything Marx published

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:10.840
as opposed to just wrote down
in notebooks or manuscripts,

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:13.520
had to get through a censor.

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:16.330
And quite a lot of his life
he was trying to get published

00:35:16.330 --> 00:35:17.803
and dodged censors,

00:35:19.133 --> 00:35:22.350
and had all his difficulties with that.

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:25.810
And indeed the newspaper
that he joined in 1842

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:30.320
was closed down by the censorship.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.440
So this is an ongoing issue and a problem.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:36.580
It follows that if he
wanted to say anything

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:40.733
much about politics other
than how good the rulers were,

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:45.860
you were going to be
scrutinized very heavily.

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:48.560
And the rigorous and the
religious establishment

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:50.903
were all entirely the same people.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:55.410
So you criticize their religion,

00:35:55.410 --> 00:35:58.160
you overthrow society,

00:35:58.160 --> 00:35:59.490
you upset politics.

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:01.790
There's certainly no
division of anything there,

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.930
quite the opposite.

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:05.800
So they're always kind of sniffing along

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:09.213
on those lines as well.

00:36:16.403 --> 00:36:19.820
"Capital" had to get
around the censor of like

00:36:19.820 --> 00:36:24.599
all of Marx's works and indeed
those of his contemporaries,

00:36:24.599 --> 00:36:28.620
the people he is associated
with and writing with.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:32.780
They write it for publication
in a kind of code,

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:35.740
it's got to be coded as a philosophy

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:38.270
or eventually politically economy.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:41.540
I think it's hard to
make people understand

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:43.800
that Marx writes about Hegel

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:46.880
not because he wants to
have a philosophical battle

00:36:46.880 --> 00:36:48.480
with a dead philosopher,

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.880
but because Hegel is a political issue,

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:53.240
and Hegel is a political issue

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:58.240
because you can appear
to talk about philosophy

00:36:58.280 --> 00:37:02.520
and actually in a coded way,
be talking about politics.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:05.280
So you could get that kind
of thing through the censor

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:10.060
and through the academic authorities,

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:13.250
who of course made sure that

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:16.040
conservative professors were appointed

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:18.310
to give official lectures on Hegel

00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:20.790
and the more radical commentary

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:25.090
comes in the alternative publications,

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:27.640
and that's the kind of
world that Marx grew up in

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:31.237
or got to know himself in the 1840s.

00:37:32.690 --> 00:37:36.750
So we've got that first
difficulty with "Capital."

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:38.353
What kind of coding is it?

00:37:39.200 --> 00:37:41.637
It's very ironic.

00:37:41.637 --> 00:37:44.623
"Capital" itself is a satire.

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:50.393
He's setting the political
economist up for a fore.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:55.500
He's fooling us really.

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:57.910
I mean, those of us in the know would know

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:01.180
that he's going to pull
the rug out from under them

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:05.210
and there is a critical work
in a very serious sense,

00:38:05.210 --> 00:38:07.790
and it's not a minor criticism.

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:09.530
But to get this past the censor

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:14.530
and indeed to get a
gullible audience on board,

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:18.550
particularly among the
professors as he said,

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:21.470
it's got to look like a sober, serious,

00:38:21.470 --> 00:38:24.670
a (speaks in foreign
language) scientific work

00:38:24.670 --> 00:38:26.550
of political economy.

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:29.910
And indeed to a certain extent,

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:32.120
he thinks they were onto something

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:34.920
and got it right in certain respects

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:37.070
even if they didn't like the politics.

00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:39.900
But they also got it wrong in a way

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:41.610
that he thinks is really interesting,

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.650
and that he can do things with.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.800
So there's an element of parody here.

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:47.513
It's a very good parody.

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:50.830
It is a difficulty with parodies

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:52.960
that if they're too good,

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:56.660
the audience won't get
the satirical difference

00:38:56.660 --> 00:39:01.660
between the parody satire
and the real thing.

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:07.510
So there are two reasons there
why it's coded as a parody.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:09.560
One is to get it past the censor,

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:11.870
the other is to get an audience on board

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:15.900
and then whoops, pull the
rug out from under them

00:39:15.900 --> 00:39:19.470
which he does very neatly
in the final chapters,

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:23.133
on part eight, chapters 26 to 33.

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:30.230
So, I really argue that for
all these reasons and others,

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:35.960
capitalism is a genre by itself.

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:39.840
It's not going to fit history
or sociology or philosophy.

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:42.680
You've got to kind of get
on board with the author

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:44.580
who's setting his own genre

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:47.440
and his own kind of context.

00:39:47.440 --> 00:39:50.610
But he's in difficulties
trying to explain to you

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:54.063
particularly if you live 100,

00:39:56.482 --> 00:39:58.693
60, 70, 80 years later.

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.240
That, I mean he's not gonna know that.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:06.490
So there is that element of difficulty.

00:40:06.490 --> 00:40:10.240
The political economy,
the economics of the times

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:13.710
is of course quite different
from modern economics.

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:16.360
And here, Marx got a kind of rug

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:17.850
pulled out from under himself.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:20.630
In the later 1870s there
was what was called

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:24.270
the marginalist revolution in economics

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:30.480
and the economists were
pretty suddenly thrown over

00:40:31.877 --> 00:40:34.460
as passe and unscientific,

00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:38.270
not doing the right kind
of thing in the right way.

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:41.190
Obsessed with value

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:46.110
and with a kind of
philosophical moral problem,

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:49.140
which was where this profit come from.

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:50.730
Whereas in the marginalist world,

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:52.890
I mean, there really isn't
the problem about profit.

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:55.590
The only problem is really

00:40:55.590 --> 00:40:57.480
how do people make more of it

00:40:58.810 --> 00:41:03.420
and how to do that with
numbers and the empirical data,

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:04.637
roughly speaking.

00:41:04.637 --> 00:41:07.290
And to move out of the
sphere of production

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:10.860
where Marx and the
political economists were

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:14.120
in the first instance into
the fear of consumption.

00:41:14.120 --> 00:41:18.840
So modern marginalist
economics really starts

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:21.163
from the point of view
of the consumer with,

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:25.827
consumers with money in their
hands ready to buy things

00:41:25.827 --> 00:41:29.220
and assume that that is
going to send a message

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:33.760
to producers via this mechanism.

00:41:33.760 --> 00:41:35.530
So the whole thing is really turned

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:37.323
upside down and inside out.

00:41:38.442 --> 00:41:42.120
And Marx I don't think ever,
ever realized that exactly.

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:44.063
It was just something,

00:41:44.063 --> 00:41:45.007
it was really quite different

00:41:45.007 --> 00:41:48.690
and from his perspective
didn't make any sense.

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:52.670
So trying to translate a
critique of political economy

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:56.310
to make it accessible for modern
readers is quite a stretch

00:41:56.310 --> 00:42:00.500
so I congratulate the two
Pauls in taking this on.

00:42:00.500 --> 00:42:04.860
It'll be fascinating to read the result.

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:08.930
So, I'm not quite sure
how I'm doing for time.

00:42:08.930 --> 00:42:12.530
Paul North, can you
give me a clue on this?

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:15.050
- If you're feeling like
you're coming to an end,

00:42:15.050 --> 00:42:17.080
there's probably a lot of questions.

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:18.280
- Okay.

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:21.550
- And you can fill in the details

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:23.650
in between people's questions.

00:42:23.650 --> 00:42:25.970
So thank you for a great presentation

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:30.250
and especially for setting
"Capital" and "Marx"

00:42:30.250 --> 00:42:34.130
into his context and its context.

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:36.300
That's something that really was forgotten

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:38.967
often in readings of "Capital."

00:42:39.910 --> 00:42:41.830
I wanna give the first question

00:42:41.830 --> 00:42:45.023
to my collaborator, Paul Reitter.

00:42:49.240 --> 00:42:51.010
Go ahead, Paul.
- Thanks.

00:42:51.010 --> 00:42:52.670
Terrell, it's great to meet you.

00:42:52.670 --> 00:42:55.400
Thank you for the presentation.

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:58.130
We've actually been in
touch a little bit as well

00:42:58.130 --> 00:43:03.130
over the summer and Terrell
is a translator of "Marx" too.

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:07.940
And so, when he talks about

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:10.690
the process of translating
"Marx" with an air of

00:43:10.690 --> 00:43:12.670
sympathetic weariness or something,

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:14.513
you know where it comes from.

00:43:15.590 --> 00:43:17.250
But he's produced some great translations

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:18.840
and I admire his translations

00:43:18.840 --> 00:43:22.230
and I have read a bit about this argument

00:43:22.230 --> 00:43:27.200
that "Capital" is, in a
fundamental way, a satire.

00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:29.450
And I wanted to ask you about that

00:43:29.450 --> 00:43:30.530
to follow up a little bit

00:43:30.530 --> 00:43:32.720
because one thing that I've noticed

00:43:32.720 --> 00:43:35.350
in doing the translation is that

00:43:35.350 --> 00:43:39.860
when Marx translates passages

00:43:39.860 --> 00:43:43.010
from political economy into German,

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:45.750
he engages in what you might call

00:43:45.750 --> 00:43:48.453
a strongly domesticating
form of translation.

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:53.890
Meaning that he really
integrates their writing into his

00:43:53.890 --> 00:43:58.300
so that stylistically
there's not much difference

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:02.780
between the quotations in
his German and his German.

00:44:02.780 --> 00:44:07.410
And you would think that
if he were looking to

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:11.763
identify this or treat this
as the object of his satire,

00:44:13.158 --> 00:44:14.800
that he would perhaps in some way,

00:44:14.800 --> 00:44:17.410
keep it at arm's length
from his own style,

00:44:17.410 --> 00:44:20.670
and the style in "Capital"
most of the time,

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:22.420
it certainly not,

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:23.800
I don't have the sense that it's meant

00:44:23.800 --> 00:44:25.340
to be a satirical style.

00:44:25.340 --> 00:44:27.760
It seems to be something that he really

00:44:27.760 --> 00:44:30.670
tried to make into an effective instrument

00:44:30.670 --> 00:44:33.670
for conveying his ideas.

00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:36.090
It's a really interesting style,

00:44:36.090 --> 00:44:37.720
a style that's very different

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:40.400
from 19th century German academic prose.

00:44:40.400 --> 00:44:43.890
I mean, it's marked in various ways by,

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:46.980
as coming from that time
for all sorts of references.

00:44:46.980 --> 00:44:49.511
I'm sorry about the
dog, barking dog there.

00:44:49.511 --> 00:44:51.600
(laughs)
Pleasures of Zoom.

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:56.350
But it also syntactically
and in terms of word choices,

00:44:56.350 --> 00:44:58.450
it's so different from
something like Wonka.

00:44:58.450 --> 00:44:59.440
It has this leanness,

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:01.200
the sleekness to it a lot of the time.

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:03.270
And he gives this political economy

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:05.290
that's often really windy and florid

00:45:05.290 --> 00:45:09.410
and gives it a kind of
leanness and tautness

00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:13.390
that it doesn't have,
doesn't necessarily deserve.

00:45:13.390 --> 00:45:18.390
And so, I'm wondering how that
connects with this argument

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:22.657
about the satirical agenda in "Capital."

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:26.513
- Well, that's an extremely good question.

00:45:28.030 --> 00:45:31.900
He's doing a couple of things at once.

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:34.250
It's not as straightforward satire.

00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:36.750
And we know from the 1840s

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:40.020
that he could write
straightforward ad hominem,

00:45:40.020 --> 00:45:41.980
sarcastic satires.

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:43.460
We know that from "The Holy Family"

00:45:43.460 --> 00:45:45.630
and "The Poverty of a Philosophy."

00:45:45.630 --> 00:45:49.040
And we know that from the
kind of ad hominem stuff

00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:51.370
that was going into the manuscripts

00:45:51.370 --> 00:45:54.063
that never actually had a title 'til 1902

00:45:55.070 --> 00:45:58.060
when they became known
as "The German Ideology."

00:45:58.060 --> 00:46:00.890
So we know he was very adapted that

00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:04.240
and he's not doing the
same thing in "Capital."

00:46:04.240 --> 00:46:05.913
It's a parody.

00:46:08.504 --> 00:46:11.613
So it's going to sneak up on the reader.

00:46:12.982 --> 00:46:15.120
And so as the text progresses,

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:18.820
you get more and more kind
of sarcastic comments,

00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:21.030
particularly in footnotes.

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:25.350
But he's also at the same time

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:27.550
finding their work straightforward

00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:30.446
and valuable descriptively.

00:46:30.446 --> 00:46:32.840
That is they are working
with the concepts of

00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:35.110
ordinary people and ordinary commodity,

00:46:35.110 --> 00:46:37.460
traders and merchants,

00:46:37.460 --> 00:46:39.760
and ordinary readers of newspapers

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:41.620
like the one that he worked for,

00:46:41.620 --> 00:46:43.750
Politics, Trade, and Industry.

00:46:43.750 --> 00:46:46.350
And what he is doing is

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:49.270
writing both of these
things at the same time

00:46:49.270 --> 00:46:52.140
as indeed the political economists were.

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:54.513
They weren't importing concepts.

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:59.510
They were reframing and
clarifying the concepts

00:46:59.510 --> 00:47:02.060
that were already in ordinary usage.

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:04.020
So Marx is quite onboard with that

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:07.730
because he's looking at
this clarified version

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:12.533
of what actually goes on in
commodity producing a society.

00:47:12.533 --> 00:47:16.480
In a sense, he's actually buying into

00:47:16.480 --> 00:47:18.480
the puzzle about profit

00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:23.090
and he's just coming up with
a new concept and a solution

00:47:23.090 --> 00:47:24.410
that they hadn't come up with.

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:26.650
But he's coming up with a solution

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:29.277
that will pull the rug
out from under them,

00:47:29.277 --> 00:47:33.380
and the solution of course
is the exploitation of labor,

00:47:33.380 --> 00:47:35.440
the production of surplus value.

00:47:35.440 --> 00:47:40.440
And that production as
the foundation of capital

00:47:42.540 --> 00:47:45.430
as self-expanding value.

00:47:45.430 --> 00:47:50.430
So it's a very difficult book in that way

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:53.630
but you may disagree,

00:47:53.630 --> 00:47:58.630
but I always find his
prose very straightforward,

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:01.090
very lean, very taut.

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:06.090
Sometimes, well, extremely
sharp, very combative.

00:48:07.470 --> 00:48:11.890
I read all of Marx as
political intervention

00:48:11.890 --> 00:48:15.150
from early 1842.

00:48:15.150 --> 00:48:17.233
And I mean, all of it.

00:48:18.140 --> 00:48:20.530
He could write the kind of

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:23.040
intellectualized journalism

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:24.440
that they had to write at the time

00:48:24.440 --> 00:48:28.123
to get into the paper and to
communicate to that audience.

00:48:29.150 --> 00:48:33.840
But it's not as difficult
as later 19th century

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:37.840
and certainly 20th
century academic German.

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:39.730
And certainly Marx's prose,

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:43.000
I mean, his best prose work for me

00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:45.370
is "The Eighteenth Brumaire
of Louis Bonaparte."

00:48:45.370 --> 00:48:47.580
I think it's a fantastic text

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:51.530
and it's the worst translation
in this so-called standard,

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:56.410
ones in true English that there
is done by Daniel De Leon.

00:48:56.410 --> 00:48:59.220
So I really enjoyed re-translating that

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:04.310
and it's really explosive

00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:06.763
and at the same time colorful.

00:49:08.204 --> 00:49:11.270
I think he's a fantastic writer in prose.

00:49:11.270 --> 00:49:13.140
If I had to pick something

00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:16.020
and my knowledge is not where yours is,

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:19.480
as a kind of model, I would
have picked the Lutheran Bible.

00:49:19.480 --> 00:49:23.623
I think that's actually direct stuff.

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:26.930
- Thank you, Terrell.

00:49:26.930 --> 00:49:27.850
Thank you, Paul.

00:49:27.850 --> 00:49:32.850
We want to, I think there's
a lot of explosive ideas

00:49:32.930 --> 00:49:35.260
in what you've been saying
for the past hour or so,

00:49:35.260 --> 00:49:37.180
including that all of Marx's writing

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:41.220
is a political intervention,
not a science, for example.

00:49:41.220 --> 00:49:44.110
Not metaphysics for another example.

00:49:44.110 --> 00:49:45.890
And we can talk about that.

00:49:45.890 --> 00:49:49.010
I want to just reiterate what this is

00:49:49.010 --> 00:49:50.700
for those who may have come in later.

00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:52.540
This is all of our chance to be

00:49:52.540 --> 00:49:55.830
TV, radio announcers I guess

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:58.340
and none of us have trained for it.

00:49:58.340 --> 00:50:01.490
We should keep in mind here
that we're being subsumed

00:50:01.490 --> 00:50:06.220
into the apparatus in a
way that Marx described

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:08.470
and others have described
really eloquently.

00:50:08.470 --> 00:50:11.800
And the conditions of
our intellectual work

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:13.480
have really changed a lot.

00:50:13.480 --> 00:50:16.650
It is a real pleasure to
see how many people are here

00:50:16.650 --> 00:50:18.493
and I wanna welcome you all.

00:50:19.730 --> 00:50:23.030
You will notice that we've
jumped into the middle

00:50:23.030 --> 00:50:24.830
and a lot of us take for granted

00:50:24.830 --> 00:50:28.630
that we've read "Capital" or
know some things about Marx.

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:32.080
Terrell gave it a terrific
introduction to the context

00:50:32.080 --> 00:50:33.440
which is often forgotten.

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:36.150
And the arguments we haven't
really touched on today

00:50:36.150 --> 00:50:37.920
but he knows them very well

00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:39.640
so you're welcome to ask him

00:50:39.640 --> 00:50:43.763
questions about value or about labor.

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:46.910
What I'd like to do is

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:50.700
I'd like to give the
students in this seminar

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:53.100
that is a regular college seminar

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:55.150
that goes along with this workshop series

00:50:55.150 --> 00:50:56.913
a chance to ask some questions.

00:50:57.810 --> 00:51:00.250
Students, if you want to begin.

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:02.290
If you put your questions in the chat,

00:51:02.290 --> 00:51:04.400
I will then somehow get to you.

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:06.610
We'll unmute you and you can ask

00:51:06.610 --> 00:51:08.810
Professor Carver yourselves.

00:51:08.810 --> 00:51:10.500
Then after that we'll open up

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:14.210
and I'll let you know for
the rest of those listening

00:51:15.190 --> 00:51:16.920
when you can put your
questions in the chat.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:19.683
We really want to try to
get to as many as possible.

00:51:20.760 --> 00:51:22.240
So for students in the class

00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:26.210
I have already one in an email.

00:51:26.210 --> 00:51:27.470
This is from, I'm gonna read it

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:29.970
since I have it here from Avery Youngblood

00:51:29.970 --> 00:51:31.667
who's a student in the School of Art.

00:51:31.667 --> 00:51:35.350
"I wonder what it means to
quote unquote, "shop small,"

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:37.450
a.k.a. support your local businesses

00:51:37.450 --> 00:51:39.920
in terms of Marx's views
on private property

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:43.420
and workers versus non-workers."

00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:46.090
There we go, right to
the heart of the matter.

00:51:46.090 --> 00:51:47.270
- Yes, I agree.

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:49.774
Private property is
the heart of the matter

00:51:49.774 --> 00:51:52.200
and that's just something
that Marx puts his finger on

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:55.020
already in 1842.

00:51:55.020 --> 00:51:56.400
There was some magic moments

00:51:56.400 --> 00:51:58.860
in his early newspaper articles

00:51:58.860 --> 00:52:03.607
and this kind of brings me
on to my approach to the text

00:52:04.940 --> 00:52:06.020
for the modern reader

00:52:06.020 --> 00:52:08.123
which is really to start at the end.

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:11.340
To start in part eight

00:52:11.340 --> 00:52:16.340
the so-called primitive
accumulation, chapters 26 to 33,

00:52:16.537 --> 00:52:18.300
'cause I think those are the ones

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:21.280
that make the most sense at the moment.

00:52:21.280 --> 00:52:23.930
But to make sense of those you have to see

00:52:23.930 --> 00:52:26.200
as he explains in those chapters,

00:52:26.200 --> 00:52:28.470
the centrality of the developments

00:52:28.470 --> 00:52:30.580
of modern private property.

00:52:30.580 --> 00:52:32.870
So the story for Marx begins in 1842

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:37.250
with an article on the dispossession

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:42.250
of feudalant peasants from
forestry where they had rights,

00:52:45.780 --> 00:52:48.760
subsistence rights to do various things,

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:52.350
pick up firewood or
whatever whilst they needed.

00:52:52.350 --> 00:52:55.880
And by actively local ruler,

00:52:55.880 --> 00:52:58.830
this was going to be privatized,

00:52:58.830 --> 00:53:01.670
turned into a piece of private property,

00:53:01.670 --> 00:53:05.830
sold off to an owner who would
subdivide it and make farms.

00:53:05.830 --> 00:53:10.390
And they would all be stuck
uncompensated of course.

00:53:10.390 --> 00:53:14.540
So it's this process of dispossession

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:18.060
through which modern
private property is created

00:53:19.400 --> 00:53:24.400
that sets commodity
production in motion for Marx

00:53:27.140 --> 00:53:32.140
such that workers become
available to employers

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:36.590
because they haven't
got anywhere else to go.

00:53:36.590 --> 00:53:38.830
And they can't go anywhere else

00:53:38.830 --> 00:53:40.680
'cause they're denied access to the land

00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:44.630
so therefore they have to work for wages

00:53:44.630 --> 00:53:49.630
and go into shops to buy processed food

00:53:51.530 --> 00:53:55.290
which is then are produced
for profits are elsewhere

00:53:55.290 --> 00:53:59.560
in agribusiness and
packaging and processing.

00:53:59.560 --> 00:54:03.970
So this is essentially
what's already going on

00:54:03.970 --> 00:54:06.790
in England and Scotland
in the late 18th century

00:54:06.790 --> 00:54:09.830
through enclosures and dispossession.

00:54:09.830 --> 00:54:13.650
And what is going on in the colonies

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:15.860
and including Ireland,

00:54:15.860 --> 00:54:18.810
plantations were set up in Ireland

00:54:18.810 --> 00:54:21.150
and Virginia at the same time

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:24.790
where labor costs were
driven as low as possible.

00:54:24.790 --> 00:54:29.790
And in the case of imported
forced labor to North America

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:31.493
driven down to zero.

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:33.970
So we know what that is.

00:54:33.970 --> 00:54:36.530
So, property is the crucial concept

00:54:36.530 --> 00:54:41.530
and Marx's political
intervention is to try

00:54:42.190 --> 00:54:46.990
to wake people up to what
is underneath the normality

00:54:46.990 --> 00:54:48.970
of clicking on Amazon,

00:54:48.970 --> 00:54:52.430
of going into a shop with a chip and pin,

00:54:52.430 --> 00:54:55.650
or however they are, wireless connectivity

00:54:55.650 --> 00:54:56.930
or an Apple Watch.

00:54:56.930 --> 00:55:01.930
And how the local global distinction

00:55:03.530 --> 00:55:05.483
is itself a misleading one.

00:55:07.000 --> 00:55:11.240
I was reflecting on
this only this morning.

00:55:11.240 --> 00:55:14.970
And so I'm thinking about
the supermarket where I go

00:55:14.970 --> 00:55:18.220
and I thought, what are
the flags that you see?

00:55:18.220 --> 00:55:21.290
Why do I see only Union
Jacks and buy British?

00:55:21.290 --> 00:55:23.680
And it tastes better because

00:55:23.680 --> 00:55:25.970
like tomatoes tastes better
because they're British.

00:55:25.970 --> 00:55:28.060
I mean, please.

00:55:28.060 --> 00:55:31.320
You know, if we had little flags all over

00:55:31.320 --> 00:55:33.860
all those other packets and labels,

00:55:33.860 --> 00:55:36.540
particularly indicating supply chains,

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:39.867
we'd have a much better picture of how,

00:55:42.930 --> 00:55:45.320
I'm avoiding the word ideological,

00:55:45.320 --> 00:55:50.320
but how mystificatory the local global

00:55:50.320 --> 00:55:52.730
consumer's position is

00:55:52.730 --> 00:55:54.890
and how it takes away from the actual

00:55:54.890 --> 00:55:57.140
brutal hard facts of production

00:55:57.140 --> 00:56:00.370
which is really where Marx is starting.

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:02.810
- Terrell, are you saying,
if I can just follow up,

00:56:02.810 --> 00:56:07.480
that the local is a fiction

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:10.580
and that really the supply
chains can't be avoided

00:56:10.580 --> 00:56:12.140
when producing something like that?

00:56:12.140 --> 00:56:14.380
Or that the local is such a small fraction

00:56:14.380 --> 00:56:17.253
of the world economy that
it's inconsequential?

00:56:18.289 --> 00:56:19.790
- It's not a fiction, it's real.

00:56:19.790 --> 00:56:21.990
It's real because people use the words

00:56:21.990 --> 00:56:23.450
and they believe in the words,

00:56:23.450 --> 00:56:27.330
and they organize their
lives around these words.

00:56:27.330 --> 00:56:31.490
But it's mystificatory
and this is going straight

00:56:31.490 --> 00:56:35.540
from Marx's playbook "Capital,"

00:56:35.540 --> 00:56:38.770
a section on fetishism of commodities,

00:56:38.770 --> 00:56:40.433
chapter one, section four.

00:56:41.440 --> 00:56:45.240
I believe you can read
the real stuff there

00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:50.240
where he describes how economics

00:56:51.410 --> 00:56:53.920
and the political economy
of his day and ours

00:56:53.920 --> 00:56:58.270
is a political project to
ensure that we see something is

00:56:58.270 --> 00:57:00.110
and don't reflect on others.

00:57:00.110 --> 00:57:01.890
So it will hit, I don't know,

00:57:01.890 --> 00:57:04.410
The Huffington Post and
the news sites occasionally

00:57:04.410 --> 00:57:06.890
that Apple workers in Shenzhen

00:57:08.020 --> 00:57:10.120
have terrible working conditions.

00:57:10.120 --> 00:57:11.990
Well, what should we think about that?

00:57:11.990 --> 00:57:15.200
And what Marx is providing
or trying to provide

00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:18.660
is a framework through
which to understand that

00:57:18.660 --> 00:57:20.600
and to form a judgment.

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:25.600
And he makes very clear
with sarcastic comments

00:57:25.640 --> 00:57:30.640
and eventually a thinly
disguised programmatic one

00:57:30.680 --> 00:57:33.910
in chapter 27 I think

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:38.360
where he's coming from and
what he thinks the solution is.

00:57:38.360 --> 00:57:40.420
But it's got to get past the censor

00:57:40.420 --> 00:57:43.763
and we all have issues with that.

00:57:45.040 --> 00:57:46.210
- Thank you.

00:57:46.210 --> 00:57:50.020
I have two further questions
from students in the seminar

00:57:50.020 --> 00:57:54.410
starting with Bilal who
asks Professor Carver,

00:57:54.410 --> 00:57:56.550
well, let's see if Bilal can unmute.

00:57:56.550 --> 00:57:58.973
Can we unmute Bilal, let
him speak for himself?

00:58:01.320 --> 00:58:05.203
This may require some technical magic.

00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:08.640
- [Yasmin] Yeah, Paul,
before we get started,

00:58:08.640 --> 00:58:10.890
there's a couple of questions
above if you scroll up.

00:58:10.890 --> 00:58:13.390
So there's one from a Jonas
and then a Ryan Ofman.

00:58:14.250 --> 00:58:16.660
So Jonas Kilga also had a question above.

00:58:16.660 --> 00:58:17.493
- Good.

00:58:17.493 --> 00:58:19.570
Let's since I am going backwards,

00:58:19.570 --> 00:58:21.300
let's stick with Bilal for the first,

00:58:21.300 --> 00:58:22.612
if you can unmute him.
- [Yasmin] Okay.

00:58:22.612 --> 00:58:25.260
- [Paul] Then we'll just go
backwards through the list.

00:58:25.260 --> 00:58:26.093
Thank you.

00:58:29.860 --> 00:58:30.693
- Right.

00:58:30.693 --> 00:58:31.773
Thank you, Professor Carver.

00:58:31.773 --> 00:58:33.490
I had a question.

00:58:33.490 --> 00:58:36.040
From your text you'd mentioned
other contemporary texts

00:58:36.040 --> 00:58:38.649
that share the title "Capital"

00:58:38.649 --> 00:58:39.970
and have a very similar approach.

00:58:39.970 --> 00:58:41.379
And you mentioned the Piketty's

00:58:41.379 --> 00:58:44.130
"Capital in The Twenty-First Century."

00:58:44.130 --> 00:58:45.130
There are some...

00:58:45.130 --> 00:58:46.610
I mean, I saw similarities between

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:50.440
Piketty's critique of capitalism
and Marx's "Capital" itself

00:58:50.440 --> 00:58:52.225
but the methods are quite different.

00:58:52.225 --> 00:58:53.793
It's not a really a
traditional Marxist approach.

00:58:53.793 --> 00:58:56.030
So I was wondering how Marx would react,

00:58:56.030 --> 00:58:58.630
his findings about inequality
in the 21st century

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:00.363
and the world that he describes.

00:59:02.000 --> 00:59:03.810
- Well, I think Marx would
definitely buy the book

00:59:03.810 --> 00:59:08.040
and buy the next book about
ideology and capitalism

00:59:08.040 --> 00:59:13.040
which essentially says what I've just said

00:59:13.370 --> 00:59:15.003
in the previous answer.

00:59:15.870 --> 00:59:18.930
That is a lot of effort goes
into helping us see things

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:20.870
one way rather than another.

00:59:20.870 --> 00:59:23.330
And not connecting the dots you might say

00:59:23.330 --> 00:59:26.630
so I see Piketty in both books

00:59:26.630 --> 00:59:29.820
as trying to connect up the dots

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.670
that Marx said we're there

00:59:31.670 --> 00:59:33.840
but doing it with modern methods

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:36.790
and particularly with
an absolute avalanche

00:59:36.790 --> 00:59:41.610
of empirical examples, data
statistics, and all of that,

00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:44.830
I think Marx was trying
to get his hands on that

00:59:44.830 --> 00:59:45.897
but of course in 1867

00:59:47.890 --> 00:59:51.620
statistics data collection
really hardly exist.

00:59:51.620 --> 00:59:54.350
So I think one instructive
thing in reading a text

00:59:54.350 --> 00:59:55.720
from out of the past like this

00:59:55.720 --> 01:00:00.720
is to spend some time
trying to think about

01:00:00.897 --> 01:00:03.840
and to find out about what sources

01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:08.840
and what knowledge of was
even potentially available

01:00:09.850 --> 01:00:11.780
to the person at that time.

01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:13.760
If you think about what Marx says

01:00:13.760 --> 01:00:15.980
about China and India for instance,

01:00:15.980 --> 01:00:18.710
how could he have known much of anything

01:00:18.710 --> 01:00:22.890
and in terms of the sources
that he did have available?

01:00:22.890 --> 01:00:24.350
What in fact were they?

01:00:24.350 --> 01:00:27.160
This is still very early days in terms of

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:31.410
even collecting data that
calls itself economic

01:00:32.580 --> 01:00:34.680
about European countries.

01:00:34.680 --> 01:00:38.980
And for as far as trying to understand

01:00:39.860 --> 01:00:42.170
societies outside Europe.

01:00:42.170 --> 01:00:45.340
I mean, this is really early days also.

01:00:45.340 --> 01:00:49.500
So there just actually
isn't very much there.

01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:51.090
I mean, even if,

01:00:51.090 --> 01:00:54.130
I remember Darwin is only 1859

01:00:55.070 --> 01:00:57.100
and that's the origin of species

01:00:57.100 --> 01:01:02.100
is built on geological
knowledge from the 1830s, 1840s

01:01:03.480 --> 01:01:05.628
on which which was not accepted.

01:01:05.628 --> 01:01:08.410
I mean, Darwin was persecuted

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:12.400
and had endless problems
for questioning the Bible.

01:01:12.400 --> 01:01:14.560
So if you look at the 1840s and ask,

01:01:14.560 --> 01:01:16.980
well, what could Marx possibly have known

01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:19.433
about the Roman Empire
or the ancient world,

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:23.160
or the ancient world
outside the Roman Empire?

01:01:23.160 --> 01:01:25.590
You're not looking at very much

01:01:25.590 --> 01:01:27.660
and quite a lot of it is gonna be located

01:01:27.660 --> 01:01:28.850
in around the Bible,

01:01:28.850 --> 01:01:31.280
they're certainly filtered through that.

01:01:31.280 --> 01:01:34.300
So I think one has to bear
all those things in mind

01:01:34.300 --> 01:01:37.047
but I think he'd be delighted
with Piketty's books.

01:01:37.047 --> 01:01:38.560
I think they're great.

01:01:38.560 --> 01:01:39.610
- Can I follow up?

01:01:39.610 --> 01:01:41.750
Bilal, thank you very much.

01:01:41.750 --> 01:01:45.130
This is a little bit more
of a provocation I think

01:01:45.130 --> 01:01:48.750
but Piketty in the first
book called "Capital"

01:01:48.750 --> 01:01:50.660
focuses on inequality

01:01:50.660 --> 01:01:52.770
and his solution is something like

01:01:52.770 --> 01:01:56.100
redistribution through taxation.

01:01:56.100 --> 01:01:58.520
Do you think if Marx had the statistics

01:01:58.520 --> 01:01:59.750
that he would come down,

01:01:59.750 --> 01:02:01.200
he would have been a liberal?

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:06.980
- Well, I think there's little difference

01:02:06.980 --> 01:02:10.400
between Marx and liberals to start with

01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:12.660
but there is some difference.

01:02:12.660 --> 01:02:14.950
So as I said, Marx is fully on board

01:02:14.950 --> 01:02:17.530
with popular sovereignty
and universal male suffrage,

01:02:17.530 --> 01:02:22.450
which was actually in the
French Constitution of 1793.

01:02:22.450 --> 01:02:25.643
They just didn't put it into practice.

01:02:26.540 --> 01:02:31.360
And as I say it was first
put into practice in 1848.

01:02:31.360 --> 01:02:35.810
So remember, it's commonly said Marx is

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:38.510
very influenced by the
French revolutionaries

01:02:38.510 --> 01:02:42.740
but he's very close to the
French revolution of 1830

01:02:42.740 --> 01:02:45.670
which is the constitutional monarchy.

01:02:45.670 --> 01:02:48.770
I mean, that's in his lifetime.

01:02:48.770 --> 01:02:50.900
So I think again, we need to need to get

01:02:51.790 --> 01:02:53.750
close to where these people actually are

01:02:53.750 --> 01:02:57.967
to understand what it is
that they're thinking about.

01:02:57.967 --> 01:03:01.270
And the suffrage is so
somewhat widened at that point.

01:03:01.270 --> 01:03:04.790
So Marx does face a political
dilemma from the beginning

01:03:04.790 --> 01:03:06.780
because as early as 1842

01:03:06.780 --> 01:03:08.920
he's onto the private property thing

01:03:09.872 --> 01:03:12.120
and the suffering of the peasants.

01:03:12.120 --> 01:03:13.920
So on the one hand,

01:03:13.920 --> 01:03:17.510
the liberal businessman would
like to get the peasants

01:03:17.510 --> 01:03:21.280
into the shops with money

01:03:22.762 --> 01:03:24.100
and the government doesn't really

01:03:24.100 --> 01:03:25.850
want them into the factories

01:03:25.850 --> 01:03:28.383
because they don't really want factories.

01:03:29.480 --> 01:03:33.610
And the charity angle isn't working out,

01:03:33.610 --> 01:03:36.110
so what the businessmen
are really upset about

01:03:36.950 --> 01:03:39.730
is the town filling up with beggars.

01:03:39.730 --> 01:03:41.310
This is going to be really annoying

01:03:41.310 --> 01:03:43.260
for people who want to shop.

01:03:43.260 --> 01:03:46.400
So that's why they want Friedrich List

01:03:46.400 --> 01:03:51.400
to come in and help
the Prussian government

01:03:51.600 --> 01:03:53.157
sort themselves out.

01:03:54.570 --> 01:03:57.920
But they don't want too much
messing with private property

01:03:57.920 --> 01:04:00.700
or too much redistribution.

01:04:00.700 --> 01:04:05.030
They just like some economic
organization of wage labor

01:04:05.990 --> 01:04:07.900
which they think would
be quite beneficial.

01:04:07.900 --> 01:04:09.600
And if it requires dispossession

01:04:11.050 --> 01:04:12.720
that's quite okay with them.

01:04:12.720 --> 01:04:16.560
And actually it's quite
okay with Marx at that point

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:18.860
since he's got to have allies.

01:04:18.860 --> 01:04:23.700
Those who believe in the
collective ownership of property

01:04:23.700 --> 01:04:27.310
rather than the ownership
of private property,

01:04:27.310 --> 01:04:31.333
particularly in the means
of production such as land,

01:04:34.121 --> 01:04:35.770
there's going to be a tension there,

01:04:35.770 --> 01:04:38.123
so he's got to keep that kind of quiet.

01:04:39.040 --> 01:04:41.180
And Moses Hess was involved with this,

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:43.600
everybody was looking for a solution.

01:04:43.600 --> 01:04:45.367
So these people have got to work together

01:04:45.367 --> 01:04:47.440
and that's what Marx did in the Rhineland,

01:04:47.440 --> 01:04:49.070
it's what he did in Paris,

01:04:49.070 --> 01:04:50.690
it's what he did in Brussels.

01:04:50.690 --> 01:04:53.170
There weren't enough socialists around

01:04:53.170 --> 01:04:54.900
and they weren't collected enough

01:04:54.900 --> 01:04:56.740
on what on earth they meant

01:04:56.740 --> 01:04:59.850
by collective ownership of property

01:04:59.850 --> 01:05:02.890
anyway to make much of a case.

01:05:02.890 --> 01:05:04.750
So they're still working that out.

01:05:04.750 --> 01:05:08.690
They're just trying to
get rid of reactionary

01:05:08.690 --> 01:05:11.913
religious, fanatic, maniac,

01:05:13.210 --> 01:05:17.520
authoritarian rulers who can
be really, really annoying,

01:05:17.520 --> 01:05:20.030
and really frustrating

01:05:20.030 --> 01:05:22.230
and don't want anybody
to vote for anything.

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:25.390
And even in the French Republic,

01:05:25.390 --> 01:05:27.880
hardly anybody voted for anything anyway.

01:05:27.880 --> 01:05:29.870
So their first angle was democracy.

01:05:29.870 --> 01:05:32.847
And if you look to the end
of "The Communist Manifesto,"

01:05:33.920 --> 01:05:36.350
part four, there's a list of demands

01:05:36.350 --> 01:05:38.970
all of which are basically liberal demands

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:42.290
including nationalization of the banks

01:05:42.290 --> 01:05:46.030
and provision of work for the disabled

01:05:46.030 --> 01:05:49.100
and provision of work for everybody.

01:05:49.100 --> 01:05:52.617
And free public education
which is unknown at the time

01:05:52.617 --> 01:05:54.630
and all of those other kinds of things.

01:05:54.630 --> 01:05:58.270
Not a word about the
abolition of private property,

01:05:58.270 --> 01:06:00.210
it's a strictly redistribution.

01:06:00.210 --> 01:06:04.340
And if you look at the
fly-posted 17 demands

01:06:05.286 --> 01:06:06.310
of the communist party

01:06:06.310 --> 01:06:11.178
which Marx and Engels
actually got fly-posted around

01:06:11.178 --> 01:06:14.370
in the German states as
opposed to the manifesto

01:06:14.370 --> 01:06:18.170
which went no where except
the Prussian Police archives.

01:06:18.170 --> 01:06:20.620
the fly-posting, which they signed

01:06:20.620 --> 01:06:23.540
which got Engels into terrible
trouble in his hometown

01:06:24.480 --> 01:06:27.420
is a straightforward
list of those demands,

01:06:27.420 --> 01:06:29.800
all of which are impeccably liberal.

01:06:29.800 --> 01:06:31.510
So you've got to have allies.

01:06:31.510 --> 01:06:35.313
I mean, Marx was politically astute.

01:06:37.887 --> 01:06:39.560
He was making political intervention.

01:06:39.560 --> 01:06:41.650
So I don't think there's a problem there.

01:06:41.650 --> 01:06:44.160
Liberals may have felt there was a problem

01:06:44.160 --> 01:06:45.363
and they were nervous.

01:06:48.440 --> 01:06:49.670
- Thanks. Thank you very much.

01:06:49.670 --> 01:06:52.413
I want to turn to Ryan Ofman.

01:06:53.840 --> 01:06:56.643
Can we unmute Ryan for his question?

01:07:01.240 --> 01:07:03.210
- [Ryan] Hello? Hello?

01:07:03.210 --> 01:07:04.420
- [Paul] Hi, Ryan. We hear you.

01:07:04.420 --> 01:07:05.380
- [Ryan] Awesome.

01:07:05.380 --> 01:07:07.100
So my other question was kind of

01:07:07.100 --> 01:07:11.090
on your description of Marx's
relationship with science.

01:07:11.090 --> 01:07:13.340
I think there was a slide on it earlier.

01:07:13.340 --> 01:07:15.710
And I very much agree that

01:07:15.710 --> 01:07:16.970
when I'm reading his descriptions

01:07:16.970 --> 01:07:18.870
I think it's a very, very like cynical

01:07:20.070 --> 01:07:20.987
view of how science works

01:07:20.987 --> 01:07:24.120
and that very well might be the case.

01:07:24.120 --> 01:07:26.360
Do you think it's more of a general,

01:07:26.360 --> 01:07:28.520
something about the nature of science

01:07:28.520 --> 01:07:30.400
that Marx thinks is fundamentally going

01:07:30.400 --> 01:07:32.120
to kind of boil down to capitalism

01:07:32.120 --> 01:07:35.220
or is it more about the
specific institutions

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:36.970
that Marx was kind of raised on?

01:07:36.970 --> 01:07:40.510
Like I'm sure the university he was at

01:07:40.510 --> 01:07:43.890
had a quite demanding and quite specific

01:07:43.890 --> 01:07:45.563
way of dealing with science.

01:07:47.500 --> 01:07:48.520
- Thank you. Great question.

01:07:48.520 --> 01:07:51.120
The university he went to
you had no science at all.

01:07:52.600 --> 01:07:54.950
So in the sense that we have it,

01:07:54.950 --> 01:07:58.260
it was a traditional
unreformed university.

01:07:58.260 --> 01:08:01.820
So I would go back to
the educational milieu

01:08:01.820 --> 01:08:05.690
of the 1820, 1830s

01:08:06.650 --> 01:08:11.650
and this is continuous to and
beyond the '48 revolutions

01:08:14.430 --> 01:08:18.283
and it's quite an intellectual
revolution led from Berlin,

01:08:19.603 --> 01:08:23.900
and from the Prussian
royal university there

01:08:23.900 --> 01:08:27.543
and it's not from their
traditional universities like bond.

01:08:30.350 --> 01:08:35.130
(speaks in foreign language)
In the German at the time.

01:08:35.130 --> 01:08:37.300
This is a rigorous study of anything.

01:08:37.300 --> 01:08:41.040
I mean, Hegel's system
building on any subject

01:08:41.040 --> 01:08:43.830
whether metaphysics or nature

01:08:43.830 --> 01:08:47.080
or logic was (speaks in foreign language)

01:08:47.080 --> 01:08:48.863
because it was rigorous.

01:08:49.770 --> 01:08:53.760
And indeed modern science
accepts the same thing,

01:08:53.760 --> 01:08:57.250
it's supposed to be rigorous and logical

01:08:57.250 --> 01:08:58.640
in the first instance.

01:08:58.640 --> 01:09:03.640
So what you get from the
more sort of Anglocentric

01:09:07.332 --> 01:09:10.827
and later reformed German universities

01:09:12.820 --> 01:09:16.050
is conception of science that accepts

01:09:16.050 --> 01:09:18.010
a physical materialism

01:09:18.010 --> 01:09:23.010
which can only really get to
be respectable after Darwin

01:09:23.840 --> 01:09:26.540
and people have got away with

01:09:26.540 --> 01:09:30.200
a kind of separation in metaphysical terms

01:09:30.200 --> 01:09:35.200
between faith and the material world.

01:09:35.230 --> 01:09:40.003
And as that is becoming more
technologically successful,

01:09:42.561 --> 01:09:45.900
engineering and early
physical and material science

01:09:45.900 --> 01:09:47.310
were all the same thing anyway,

01:09:47.310 --> 01:09:50.500
electricity, physics,
and all of that stuff.

01:09:50.500 --> 01:09:52.800
If you go back through the

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:55.360
history of science and technology,

01:09:55.360 --> 01:09:56.900
they're all the same thing from about

01:09:56.900 --> 01:10:00.370
the 1760s, 1780s onwards.

01:10:00.370 --> 01:10:02.380
So, these things all have to have a use

01:10:02.380 --> 01:10:06.793
and the experimentation
has to be paid for.

01:10:07.658 --> 01:10:11.063
And it's often quite difficult to do.

01:10:12.040 --> 01:10:15.860
I would approach this
very much historically

01:10:15.860 --> 01:10:20.520
and try to make sure that
we're not projecting a kind of

01:10:20.520 --> 01:10:22.880
scientific materialism,

01:10:22.880 --> 01:10:25.170
matter in motion and mathematical methods

01:10:25.170 --> 01:10:28.560
from the 1870s and 1880s and onwards

01:10:28.560 --> 01:10:31.730
back onto something
that really didn't exist

01:10:31.730 --> 01:10:36.730
anywhere very much and in Marx's time.

01:10:39.430 --> 01:10:43.460
So, I'll just pull the 1844
manuscripts out of a hat here.

01:10:43.460 --> 01:10:48.420
This is another sort of
editorial kind of geeky point.

01:10:48.420 --> 01:10:51.620
These are kind of ripped
out of Marx's notebooks

01:10:51.620 --> 01:10:56.430
and turned into a manuscript
work in the 1920s.

01:10:56.430 --> 01:11:01.210
But even so, you can see
that he actually just says

01:11:02.770 --> 01:11:06.250
that in so far as we have
(speaks in foreign language),

01:11:06.250 --> 01:11:09.670
study of the material world,

01:11:09.670 --> 01:11:14.670
it's driven by and goes into
technologies of ordinary life.

01:11:17.567 --> 01:11:18.920
And that's what industry is.

01:11:18.920 --> 01:11:22.040
Marx is very fixated on technologies

01:11:22.040 --> 01:11:25.250
and therefore, economics of ordinary life

01:11:25.250 --> 01:11:29.300
in whatever stage of
society we're looking at.

01:11:29.300 --> 01:11:32.230
So it quite specifically says

01:11:32.230 --> 01:11:33.970
that his overall outlook

01:11:35.670 --> 01:11:38.900
which is historical all the way down,

01:11:38.900 --> 01:11:43.900
includes all of previous
forms and stages if you like

01:11:44.420 --> 01:11:48.670
or formations of society
in terms of the way

01:11:48.670 --> 01:11:51.160
that people interacted together

01:11:51.160 --> 01:11:54.030
to get along in the material world.

01:11:54.030 --> 01:11:57.060
It just takes different
forms at different times

01:11:57.060 --> 01:11:58.730
but he's quite happy.

01:11:58.730 --> 01:12:00.670
And this is actually in "Capital"

01:12:00.670 --> 01:12:03.820
with the stone age,
bronze age and iron age

01:12:03.820 --> 01:12:05.120
and that actually gets a footnote,

01:12:05.120 --> 01:12:10.050
and that was a new thing
in the 1860s series.

01:12:10.050 --> 01:12:14.010
Quite interested in stone age technologies

01:12:14.010 --> 01:12:17.250
and might sound odd to
call this stone age science

01:12:17.250 --> 01:12:20.170
but in terms of being
rigorous and logical,

01:12:20.170 --> 01:12:21.840
and apparently not doing what

01:12:23.540 --> 01:12:27.960
baboons or apes do when
they use simple tools.

01:12:27.960 --> 01:12:29.910
I mean, he's definitely onto something.

01:12:31.640 --> 01:12:32.590
- [Ryan] Thank you.

01:12:33.450 --> 01:12:34.500
- Thanks, Ryan.

01:12:34.500 --> 01:12:36.380
We have one more student with a question.

01:12:36.380 --> 01:12:38.340
Can we go to Jonas Kilga

01:12:39.740 --> 01:12:40.573
- [Jonas] Hi.

01:12:41.650 --> 01:12:43.200
Can everyone hear me?

01:12:43.200 --> 01:12:44.033
- Yes.
- Yeah.

01:12:44.033 --> 01:12:46.070
- [Jonas] Awesome. Thank you.

01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:50.210
So you mentioned censorship
a lot in your talk

01:12:50.210 --> 01:12:54.330
and in the text that you sent out,

01:12:54.330 --> 01:12:56.250
you also said he published "Das Kapital"

01:12:56.250 --> 01:12:58.330
in handbook originally

01:12:58.330 --> 01:13:00.863
because the censors were more lenient.

01:13:02.490 --> 01:13:03.850
And I think when you're reading Marx,

01:13:03.850 --> 01:13:07.150
it is quite a peculiar language

01:13:07.150 --> 01:13:10.060
and maybe that's just 1860s German.

01:13:10.060 --> 01:13:12.940
But knowing about the censorship

01:13:12.940 --> 01:13:14.670
or that he had to grapple with it,

01:13:14.670 --> 01:13:19.670
I was wondering concretely how "Capital"

01:13:19.890 --> 01:13:24.370
was affected by censorship
if there are any,

01:13:24.370 --> 01:13:26.700
if there's textual
evidence to look out for

01:13:26.700 --> 01:13:29.260
where you can sense that
Marx was sort of trying to

01:13:29.260 --> 01:13:34.260
write around certain stipulations
put in place by censors.

01:13:35.920 --> 01:13:37.610
- Well, I think there's
an awful lot of that.

01:13:37.610 --> 01:13:39.530
I mean, you have to put
yourself in the mind

01:13:39.530 --> 01:13:42.430
of the censor of the time

01:13:42.430 --> 01:13:45.040
and then in Marx's minds
to try to figure out

01:13:45.040 --> 01:13:46.253
what he's not writing.

01:13:47.670 --> 01:13:52.670
But that's quite a lot of
quotation from published sources,

01:13:53.380 --> 01:13:56.710
some of which is quite
critical of capitalism.

01:13:56.710 --> 01:13:59.500
So that's a move.

01:13:59.500 --> 01:14:01.850
I mean, none of these moves are guaranteed

01:14:01.850 --> 01:14:04.310
to get around the censor.

01:14:04.310 --> 01:14:08.453
I was particularly struck
in reading part eight again

01:14:08.453 --> 01:14:12.460
that we get to an apocalyptic
moments of capitalist collapse

01:14:13.410 --> 01:14:18.410
where he describes the
proletariat overthrowing

01:14:19.960 --> 01:14:21.670
the regime of private property,

01:14:21.670 --> 01:14:25.380
and says it's the
negation of the negation.

01:14:25.380 --> 01:14:27.750
Well, this is sort of pounced on in the

01:14:28.700 --> 01:14:32.480
post-1920 world of Marx reception

01:14:32.480 --> 01:14:34.513
showing that he's still valued Hegel.

01:14:35.680 --> 01:14:37.280
I think this is really a way

01:14:37.280 --> 01:14:40.870
of getting around the censorship

01:14:40.870 --> 01:14:45.870
by not saying too much about this

01:14:46.900 --> 01:14:51.320
volcanic change in the social
order that he's envisioning,

01:14:51.320 --> 01:14:53.100
and quickly covering it up

01:14:53.100 --> 01:14:57.180
with a kind of meaningless
philosophical phrase

01:14:57.180 --> 01:14:59.320
that doesn't say much of anything

01:14:59.320 --> 01:15:01.130
and then moving swiftly on.

01:15:01.130 --> 01:15:02.520
All of this,

01:15:02.520 --> 01:15:04.370
of course only comes in part eight

01:15:04.370 --> 01:15:08.163
by which time you assume the
censor has got very bored.

01:15:09.867 --> 01:15:13.410
So, censors were known to
be kind of the beginning

01:15:13.410 --> 01:15:16.490
and a bit of the end
and kind of flip through

01:15:17.465 --> 01:15:19.820
'cause they're on a paycheck,

01:15:19.820 --> 01:15:21.620
they have books to go through.

01:15:21.620 --> 01:15:25.190
So Marx was pretty successful with that

01:15:25.190 --> 01:15:27.990
and it's word of mouth where
you go for your publishers.

01:15:27.990 --> 01:15:32.160
And there's a lot of
that in correspondence

01:15:32.160 --> 01:15:35.537
about where to go and who
will put up the money for it,

01:15:35.537 --> 01:15:37.310
and how to get the money,

01:15:37.310 --> 01:15:39.470
whether you split the
cost with the publisher,

01:15:39.470 --> 01:15:42.300
and how much risk the
publisher's going to take,

01:15:42.300 --> 01:15:43.450
and all that kind of stuff.

01:15:43.450 --> 01:15:46.470
But publication was not
free and it was censored,

01:15:46.470 --> 01:15:48.420
and as I point out,

01:15:48.420 --> 01:15:51.620
they were lucky to get through
the Russian censorship.

01:15:51.620 --> 01:15:53.730
Now, at least again, in a sense

01:15:53.730 --> 01:15:54.900
on the kind of foreign angle,

01:15:54.900 --> 01:15:56.940
there's an awful lot of
foreign writers in there

01:15:56.940 --> 01:15:59.480
so we don't have to worry very much.

01:15:59.480 --> 01:16:04.370
And capitalism was a wholly foreign idea

01:16:05.636 --> 01:16:10.030
and Russia in the early
1870s, it was so new,

01:16:10.030 --> 01:16:11.720
the censor hadn't really heard of it,

01:16:11.720 --> 01:16:12.840
didn't know what to do with it.

01:16:12.840 --> 01:16:15.170
It seemed it was of no interest

01:16:15.170 --> 01:16:17.283
in a really autocratic, medieval,

01:16:18.720 --> 01:16:21.453
obsessive feudal satire.

01:16:22.540 --> 01:16:25.057
So then it was kind of a
tedious work on a subject

01:16:25.057 --> 01:16:27.320
nobody knows anything about.

01:16:27.320 --> 01:16:31.530
So you kind of have to rely
on censorial ignorance.

01:16:32.510 --> 01:16:34.480
It's not as well developed as censorship

01:16:34.480 --> 01:16:36.273
has been in other places.

01:16:38.420 --> 01:16:39.253
- Thanks, Terrell.

01:16:39.253 --> 01:16:41.670
I'm sure all the Hegelians in the audience

01:16:41.670 --> 01:16:43.497
will have been interested.

01:16:43.497 --> 01:16:45.250
(Terrell laughs)

01:16:45.250 --> 01:16:47.120
Hegel speak in the book was an attempt

01:16:47.120 --> 01:16:49.773
to get around the censors.

01:16:50.890 --> 01:16:53.550
I would like to open up
the floor for questions

01:16:53.550 --> 01:16:56.750
from all the spectators now.

01:16:56.750 --> 01:16:58.880
We have some in the chat.

01:16:58.880 --> 01:17:02.500
I wonder if we can
actually unmute the folks

01:17:02.500 --> 01:17:03.990
and have them ask their questions.

01:17:03.990 --> 01:17:04.907
Let's try.

01:17:04.907 --> 01:17:07.400
Yasmin if you're on,

01:17:07.400 --> 01:17:10.440
we have a question from Sara Wexler.

01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:13.817
I think we can find
Sara Wexler and unmute.

01:17:14.972 --> 01:17:17.070
- [Yasmin] Yes, Sara, you should
be able to unmute herself.

01:17:17.070 --> 01:17:17.903
- [Sara] Hello?

01:17:18.850 --> 01:17:20.020
- Hi. We hear you.

01:17:20.020 --> 01:17:20.853
- [Sara] Okay. Yeah.

01:17:20.853 --> 01:17:25.060
I recently read William Clare
Robert's book "Marx's Inferno"

01:17:25.060 --> 01:17:27.040
and he's also kind of trying to

01:17:27.040 --> 01:17:29.560
politically contextualize "Capital."

01:17:29.560 --> 01:17:31.170
And so I was just wondering

01:17:31.170 --> 01:17:33.280
if what you thought about his take that

01:17:34.340 --> 01:17:36.270
Marx is sort of advocating
for some sort of

01:17:36.270 --> 01:17:39.337
republicanism when he's writing "Capital."

01:17:40.930 --> 01:17:42.963
- Well, I thoroughly recommend the book.

01:17:44.029 --> 01:17:45.960
There are not a whole lot of books

01:17:45.960 --> 01:17:49.023
on this subject I recommend
but that's clearly one.

01:17:50.137 --> 01:17:53.977
I think his take on the political
intervention is wonderful.

01:17:55.180 --> 01:17:56.650
It's quite clever.

01:17:56.650 --> 01:17:59.890
He says it's written within the context

01:17:59.890 --> 01:18:03.150
of the International
Workingmen's Association

01:18:03.150 --> 01:18:05.173
to which ladies were admitted.

01:18:06.330 --> 01:18:09.900
And that it's really
getting it Proudhonist

01:18:09.900 --> 01:18:14.900
although Proudhon is hardly
mentioned in the text.

01:18:14.940 --> 01:18:19.940
So I think that's a little
bit of the parody satire.

01:18:21.270 --> 01:18:23.563
Angle Marx had already done

01:18:23.563 --> 01:18:27.303
a sort of straight up
satirical attack on Proudhon

01:18:27.303 --> 01:18:30.950
so there's not too much
point doing it again.

01:18:30.950 --> 01:18:33.110
Better just to show what he wanted to show

01:18:33.110 --> 01:18:36.770
which is that he was very much
better at it than Proudhon.

01:18:36.770 --> 01:18:39.850
And instead of riling the Proudhonians

01:18:39.850 --> 01:18:42.850
he would kind of pull a fast one on them.

01:18:42.850 --> 01:18:47.133
So I think Will is
absolutely right about that.

01:18:49.097 --> 01:18:53.650
I'm less onboard with
the republicanism thing.

01:18:53.650 --> 01:18:55.730
Quite a lot of people thought that was

01:18:55.730 --> 01:18:58.243
a little bit unnecessary or projected.

01:18:59.680 --> 01:19:02.850
I'm more inclined to stick with

01:19:02.850 --> 01:19:04.600
popular sovereignty and liberalism,

01:19:04.600 --> 01:19:07.430
although they are part
of the same dialogue.

01:19:07.430 --> 01:19:09.873
So I don't go all the way
with Will on that one.

01:19:11.430 --> 01:19:15.253
I think the Dante's "Inferno"
thing is quite clever.

01:19:16.410 --> 01:19:19.450
It shows an awful lot of really geeky,

01:19:19.450 --> 01:19:21.580
nerdy grad student research.

01:19:21.580 --> 01:19:26.530
He taught himself the
whole world of Dante ana

01:19:26.530 --> 01:19:27.730
in order to do this,

01:19:27.730 --> 01:19:32.153
so it's an awesome thing to take on board.

01:19:33.420 --> 01:19:35.860
For me it didn't actually add that much

01:19:35.860 --> 01:19:38.540
to my reading of "Capital"
to see it passed out

01:19:39.869 --> 01:19:42.270
as a kind of tribute to Dante.

01:19:42.270 --> 01:19:43.870
It's also been passed out

01:19:43.870 --> 01:19:46.133
as a kind of tribute to Hegel's logic.

01:19:47.230 --> 01:19:50.160
I think it's got a logic of its own.

01:19:50.160 --> 01:19:54.060
One of the points I make in the text

01:19:54.060 --> 01:19:57.200
is that where is "Capital"

01:19:57.200 --> 01:19:59.160
is actually quite a good question

01:19:59.160 --> 01:20:03.710
because it's a project I think from 1842

01:20:03.710 --> 01:20:08.027
and from late 1843 when he reads Engels'

01:20:08.027 --> 01:20:10.810
"Outlines of A Critique
of Political Economy"

01:20:10.810 --> 01:20:12.550
and just goes electric.

01:20:12.550 --> 01:20:16.250
He then sketches out a plan for a critique

01:20:16.250 --> 01:20:18.210
of political economy

01:20:18.210 --> 01:20:20.500
and spends the rest of his life on that.

01:20:20.500 --> 01:20:25.030
And we've got a lot of manuscript build up

01:20:25.030 --> 01:20:27.700
and reading notes and notebooks

01:20:27.700 --> 01:20:29.140
that lead up to this.

01:20:29.140 --> 01:20:34.140
And we've got the intense
activity 1857 onwards

01:20:36.250 --> 01:20:38.810
that got published
eventually as grid reserve.

01:20:38.810 --> 01:20:41.660
We've got drafts of "Capital, Volume I"

01:20:42.960 --> 01:20:46.700
and we've got his
rewrites and new additions

01:20:46.700 --> 01:20:49.703
in the French translation of it.

01:20:50.680 --> 01:20:54.500
And so therefore "Capital One"

01:20:54.500 --> 01:20:57.630
is more like a taster volume or a snapshot

01:20:57.630 --> 01:21:00.460
and we know he was kind
of pressured as usual

01:21:00.460 --> 01:21:03.140
by family and friends
to get something out.

01:21:03.140 --> 01:21:05.240
So, it's not thrown together

01:21:05.240 --> 01:21:10.240
but it's he had to make a stop with it

01:21:11.060 --> 01:21:15.370
and decide what was gonna be
in it as a single volume work.

01:21:15.370 --> 01:21:17.170
There's also a bit of a health warning

01:21:17.170 --> 01:21:19.310
on Volumes II and III.

01:21:19.310 --> 01:21:23.070
Volume III is from manuscripts
that predate Volume I.

01:21:23.070 --> 01:21:24.990
Volume II is in a very rough state

01:21:24.990 --> 01:21:27.500
and Engels' editor saying is

01:21:27.500 --> 01:21:29.860
subject to a lot of critique.

01:21:29.860 --> 01:21:31.653
Not all of it unsympathetic.

01:21:32.680 --> 01:21:34.880
Editors have a hard job when authors

01:21:34.880 --> 01:21:37.750
don't make up their mind and
say contradictory things.

01:21:37.750 --> 01:21:39.920
And I think that's a big clue about Marx.

01:21:39.920 --> 01:21:43.240
He was really curious
and really exploratory

01:21:44.450 --> 01:21:49.450
and was, didn't feel obliged
to have a dogmatic view

01:21:50.270 --> 01:21:52.170
unless he really thought about it.

01:21:52.170 --> 01:21:53.760
And then I don't think he particularly

01:21:53.760 --> 01:21:55.203
wanted to be dogmatic.

01:21:56.460 --> 01:22:00.463
- And said in a letter
that he wasn't a Marxist.

01:22:01.930 --> 01:22:05.160
- Well now I'll correct
you if I may on that.

01:22:05.160 --> 01:22:08.170
Engels' reports in two letters

01:22:08.170 --> 01:22:12.380
that he heard Marx say it
two different ways in French.

01:22:12.380 --> 01:22:13.500
Got it?

01:22:13.500 --> 01:22:14.333
- Got it.

01:22:15.170 --> 01:22:17.600
Let's turn to some other questions.

01:22:17.600 --> 01:22:20.700
I'm gonna go through the chat now.

01:22:20.700 --> 01:22:24.640
So let's see if we can unmute.

01:22:24.640 --> 01:22:26.220
- [Participant] Hi. Can everyone hear me?

01:22:26.220 --> 01:22:27.306
- Yeah.
- Absolutely.

01:22:27.306 --> 01:22:29.550
- [Paul] Okay.

01:22:29.550 --> 01:22:31.280
- [Participant] Okay, so
this is a question about

01:22:31.280 --> 01:22:32.993
the labor theory of value.

01:22:34.000 --> 01:22:35.890
I was wondering what you think

01:22:35.890 --> 01:22:37.550
about the labor theory of value

01:22:37.550 --> 01:22:42.233
and whether it has some
grain of truth to it today.

01:22:44.090 --> 01:22:46.530
- Almost everything has a grain of truth

01:22:46.530 --> 01:22:47.880
and I certainly think that.

01:22:48.940 --> 01:22:52.353
I think we have to go
back to political economy

01:22:52.353 --> 01:22:54.130
and as I've said, the puzzle

01:22:54.130 --> 01:22:55.983
that they were solving at the time,

01:22:56.960 --> 01:23:01.960
and the way that they chose
labor as a common factor.

01:23:01.980 --> 01:23:05.510
And they were presenting
a puzzle about profit

01:23:05.510 --> 01:23:08.200
and solving it or trying to solve it

01:23:08.200 --> 01:23:12.030
in very Aristotelian terms.

01:23:12.030 --> 01:23:14.880
So it's a very classical problem

01:23:14.880 --> 01:23:18.070
they're trying to solve with tools

01:23:18.070 --> 01:23:21.430
of moralizing philosophy

01:23:21.430 --> 01:23:24.710
which is somewhat connected
with the empirical world.

01:23:24.710 --> 01:23:27.280
That is they took human labor

01:23:27.280 --> 01:23:31.313
to be an empirical kind of object.

01:23:33.140 --> 01:23:38.140
That works in that kind
of neo-Aristotelian

01:23:38.730 --> 01:23:42.450
framework as far as it goes, excuse me.

01:23:42.450 --> 01:23:45.173
There's a couple of chapters
in "The Postmodern Marx"

01:23:46.610 --> 01:23:50.050
that show how it works in that context.

01:23:50.050 --> 01:23:54.660
And why if you shift your
ground to modern physics

01:23:54.660 --> 01:23:59.660
or a more post-Aristotelian, materialist,

01:24:00.130 --> 01:24:02.070
physical understanding of the world

01:24:02.070 --> 01:24:04.540
based on physics and chemistry,

01:24:04.540 --> 01:24:07.423
it really doesn't work well at all.

01:24:08.440 --> 01:24:11.840
Even just empirically, it's not clear

01:24:11.840 --> 01:24:16.000
that there's something that
we can specify or define

01:24:16.960 --> 01:24:21.067
as labor in the human
context as opposed to leisure

01:24:23.110 --> 01:24:26.093
though people talk as if there is.

01:24:27.330 --> 01:24:29.820
We can define things like
wage work and employment,

01:24:29.820 --> 01:24:33.017
ask anybody in a tax
office what employment is

01:24:33.017 --> 01:24:34.580
and they will tell you,

01:24:34.580 --> 01:24:36.760
but they probably won't
tell you what work is

01:24:36.760 --> 01:24:39.550
or what labor is.

01:24:39.550 --> 01:24:41.920
It's a convenience sort of label.

01:24:41.920 --> 01:24:45.060
One student said, well, what about donkeys

01:24:46.440 --> 01:24:47.510
and animals who work?

01:24:47.510 --> 01:24:51.720
We have a lot of hardworking
animals around the world.

01:24:51.720 --> 01:24:54.840
Why are they not since they're doing

01:24:54.840 --> 01:24:57.910
the same kinds of things
that humans are doing?

01:24:57.910 --> 01:25:02.163
We've got animals that are
working for us biologically now.

01:25:03.634 --> 01:25:05.580
And in modern terms, I don't think

01:25:05.580 --> 01:25:08.620
there are very good
answers to those questions.

01:25:08.620 --> 01:25:11.270
Alternatively, we can
turn it around and say,

01:25:11.270 --> 01:25:14.510
well, let's look at
the world of production

01:25:14.510 --> 01:25:17.350
from the point of view of the human being

01:25:17.350 --> 01:25:21.050
and what human beings are required to do

01:25:21.050 --> 01:25:23.303
because they can't subside,

01:25:24.680 --> 01:25:27.430
they can't subsist otherwise.

01:25:27.430 --> 01:25:29.480
And then I think we're much more on board

01:25:30.570 --> 01:25:32.600
with the theory of exploitation

01:25:32.600 --> 01:25:37.600
which is really where Marx's
concept of labor power

01:25:38.660 --> 01:25:41.100
and surplus value is going.

01:25:41.100 --> 01:25:43.950
He's developing a concept of exploitation.

01:25:43.950 --> 01:25:47.170
There are various ways you can construct

01:25:47.170 --> 01:25:49.340
empirical proxies for this

01:25:49.340 --> 01:25:53.290
and there's quite a lot
of mileage in doing that.

01:25:53.290 --> 01:25:58.290
Marxist economists have done
that in very respectable work

01:25:59.130 --> 01:26:01.093
which gets a very good audience.

01:26:03.383 --> 01:26:07.270
It gets ignored because
it doesn't start out

01:26:07.270 --> 01:26:08.960
with the concepts and the methods

01:26:10.164 --> 01:26:14.360
that more Orthodox economists
are used to working with.

01:26:14.360 --> 01:26:18.360
I think Piketty has a nice
point of compromise there.

01:26:18.360 --> 01:26:20.340
He's quite interested in human beings

01:26:20.340 --> 01:26:22.740
and the things that they're required to do

01:26:22.740 --> 01:26:24.323
within the productive set up

01:26:24.323 --> 01:26:29.323
that capitalism causes
them to be enmeshed in

01:26:29.810 --> 01:26:32.350
whether they like it or not,

01:26:32.350 --> 01:26:34.913
and some of them like it
and some of them don't.

01:26:36.375 --> 01:26:39.260
And so people change their views about it

01:26:39.260 --> 01:26:40.840
as life goes along,

01:26:40.840 --> 01:26:42.640
you may think it's great
when you're employed

01:26:42.640 --> 01:26:44.623
and not when you're not.

01:26:45.970 --> 01:26:48.500
So there's all those things going on.

01:26:48.500 --> 01:26:50.590
I quite liked David Harvey's approach

01:26:50.590 --> 01:26:54.690
which is really to zoom
in at Volume II stage

01:26:54.690 --> 01:26:56.470
and look at capitalist flows,

01:26:56.470 --> 01:27:01.280
and in a sense just kind of put the whole

01:27:01.280 --> 01:27:03.570
labor value thing on the back burner

01:27:04.560 --> 01:27:07.610
and look at capital flows
and then come back around

01:27:07.610 --> 01:27:10.190
to let's look at workers,

01:27:10.190 --> 01:27:12.462
and their kinds of conditions.

01:27:12.462 --> 01:27:15.410
So there are lots of
things to be done with.

01:27:15.410 --> 01:27:17.510
I think it is quite difficult

01:27:17.510 --> 01:27:21.210
to pick up "Capital, Volume
I" and start on page one

01:27:21.210 --> 01:27:23.660
and figure out what is going on,

01:27:23.660 --> 01:27:26.350
and why things are
problematic and puzzling,

01:27:26.350 --> 01:27:29.560
and what the concepts mean
and what they don't mean

01:27:29.560 --> 01:27:34.560
given what's happened in the
180 years or so afterwards.

01:27:36.360 --> 01:27:38.720
- And we don't wanna give
people too much of a fright

01:27:38.720 --> 01:27:40.867
so that they don't try it.

01:27:40.867 --> 01:27:42.690
(Terrell laughs)

01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:46.880
Your own quote I do think
should come back in here.

01:27:46.880 --> 01:27:48.177
- Okay.

01:27:48.177 --> 01:27:50.130
- It's worth it as all
difficult things are

01:27:50.130 --> 01:27:52.210
and since we have to live under capital,

01:27:52.210 --> 01:27:54.224
it can't be worse than that.

01:27:54.224 --> 01:27:55.103
(Terrell laughs)

01:27:55.103 --> 01:27:56.223
Wanna go now to a question

01:27:56.223 --> 01:28:00.540
that was posed at a little
while ago by Martin Bradbury.

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:02.110
I wonder if we can unmute Martin

01:28:02.110 --> 01:28:03.493
if he's still in the.

01:28:04.700 --> 01:28:05.890
- [Martin] Hello?

01:28:05.890 --> 01:28:06.830
- [Paul] Hi, Martin.

01:28:06.830 --> 01:28:07.840
- [Martin] Hello.

01:28:07.840 --> 01:28:12.680
So, my question is about the relationship

01:28:12.680 --> 01:28:14.850
between Hegel and Marx.

01:28:14.850 --> 01:28:16.823
Hegel's already been mentioned.

01:28:17.660 --> 01:28:22.660
And from some of my
readings of (indistinct)

01:28:22.910 --> 01:28:24.070
from the Frankfurt School,

01:28:24.070 --> 01:28:26.813
so like a dorm, they
wouldn't know if he was.

01:28:26.813 --> 01:28:29.844
I've come to sort of
understand Marx's project

01:28:29.844 --> 01:28:33.743
as basically an attempt
to radicalize Hegel.

01:28:39.167 --> 01:28:40.477
There's a section in the

01:28:40.477 --> 01:28:42.190
"Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts

01:28:42.190 --> 01:28:44.570
of course where this dedicated in time

01:28:44.570 --> 01:28:45.883
to criticizing Hegel.

01:28:45.883 --> 01:28:48.350
There's a famous quote from Marx

01:28:48.350 --> 01:28:50.870
where he talks about how when he was

01:28:50.870 --> 01:28:52.620
writing the (indistinct)

01:28:52.620 --> 01:28:55.210
he happen to leave through Hegel again.

01:28:55.210 --> 01:28:57.350
So, this is something that
keeps coming up for it,

01:28:57.350 --> 01:28:59.120
the history of Marxism.

01:28:59.120 --> 01:29:04.120
But it's also being counted
of course by other schools,

01:29:04.850 --> 01:29:05.773
the one that comes to mind spaces

01:29:05.773 --> 01:29:08.683
are the analytical Marxists.

01:29:09.870 --> 01:29:11.958
When we enable Marxist

01:29:11.958 --> 01:29:15.520
we therefore call that Hegelian
speculative philosophy.

01:29:15.520 --> 01:29:17.360
So my question really is

01:29:17.360 --> 01:29:20.910
to what extent is this idea that

01:29:20.910 --> 01:29:23.420
Marx was trying to radicalize Hegel,

01:29:23.420 --> 01:29:26.080
something that Marx would
probably have agreed with?

01:29:26.080 --> 01:29:28.880
Or is it this notion that's been

01:29:28.880 --> 01:29:32.850
brought into it by Marx's interpreters

01:29:32.850 --> 01:29:36.783
like Malki with certain
people that came afterwards.

01:29:38.150 --> 01:29:40.480
- Well, thank you very
much for that question.

01:29:40.480 --> 01:29:42.572
It's all of those things.

01:29:42.572 --> 01:29:45.340
So if we go back to the beginning,

01:29:45.340 --> 01:29:47.240
who was Hegel in the first place?

01:29:47.240 --> 01:29:49.703
Hegel was an academic philosopher.

01:29:50.910 --> 01:29:55.870
He published a lot to the
German speaking audience.

01:29:55.870 --> 01:29:58.803
He was famous as a critic of Kant.

01:29:59.660 --> 01:30:04.500
He wrote a philosophical system

01:30:04.500 --> 01:30:07.560
which was going to make
(speaks in foreign language)

01:30:07.560 --> 01:30:11.200
a rigorous sense of
everything that ever was

01:30:12.700 --> 01:30:15.010
and will be everything you could possibly

01:30:15.010 --> 01:30:17.140
want to know about.

01:30:17.140 --> 01:30:20.400
He was famous in his
own time, much admired.

01:30:20.400 --> 01:30:22.560
His disciples collected his writings

01:30:22.560 --> 01:30:27.150
and filled out his collected
works in the 1830s.

01:30:27.150 --> 01:30:29.883
And he was, as we say in Britain,

01:30:31.070 --> 01:30:34.803
by the 1830s, a national treasure.

01:30:36.170 --> 01:30:41.170
He had become the East
Prussian Philosopher of Choice.

01:30:43.080 --> 01:30:46.430
He was there in the public view.

01:30:46.430 --> 01:30:49.040
Since the public was not
allowed to have a view

01:30:49.040 --> 01:30:51.900
about very much and
there wasn't even really

01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:56.590
supposed to be a public
interested in politics

01:30:56.590 --> 01:31:01.500
because he was so famous
and so famously ambiguous,

01:31:01.500 --> 01:31:04.090
and because he covered everything

01:31:04.090 --> 01:31:07.100
including history and politics,

01:31:07.100 --> 01:31:11.070
he was away in to the
politics of the time.

01:31:11.070 --> 01:31:13.730
He was the politics of the time

01:31:13.730 --> 01:31:16.990
in that you could discuss
it in a coded form

01:31:16.990 --> 01:31:18.943
by talking about him.

01:31:20.180 --> 01:31:24.100
So, there was no need to radicalize him.

01:31:24.100 --> 01:31:29.100
He was already radicalized by the 1830s

01:31:29.430 --> 01:31:34.430
and he was already
conservativized by the 1830s.

01:31:35.440 --> 01:31:38.530
This debate was already raging

01:31:38.530 --> 01:31:42.770
and he was blamed in the
culture war of the time

01:31:42.770 --> 01:31:46.800
for destroying Christian faith

01:31:48.827 --> 01:31:51.630
and preaching atheism.

01:31:51.630 --> 01:31:54.440
But also defending the Prussian state

01:31:54.440 --> 01:31:57.700
and Orthodox confessional Christianity.

01:31:57.700 --> 01:32:01.780
So this shows you the
advantages of ambiguity

01:32:01.780 --> 01:32:04.323
and also the advantages of being dead,

01:32:05.584 --> 01:32:07.980
and of being a national treasure.

01:32:07.980 --> 01:32:10.830
So he was the national philosopher

01:32:10.830 --> 01:32:13.330
and how to read and understand him,

01:32:13.330 --> 01:32:17.070
particularly what he says about the,

01:32:17.070 --> 01:32:21.370
on political subjects and
the philosophy of right is

01:32:21.370 --> 01:32:24.020
political philosophy,
philosophy of the state

01:32:24.020 --> 01:32:26.810
and society, and the family,

01:32:26.810 --> 01:32:28.750
he's covered all those things.

01:32:28.750 --> 01:32:32.710
What you think he's saying
is itself a political issue

01:32:32.710 --> 01:32:36.170
and this is debated and published on

01:32:36.170 --> 01:32:40.510
in the Alternative Press
and in the official press,

01:32:40.510 --> 01:32:44.580
and professors are appointed by the state

01:32:44.580 --> 01:32:46.060
and kicked out by the state

01:32:46.060 --> 01:32:49.873
to take the right view on
this philosophical subject.

01:32:53.096 --> 01:32:57.263
The 20th century reception
from the 1920s onwards,

01:32:58.140 --> 01:33:00.210
doesn't quite see this.

01:33:00.210 --> 01:33:04.370
The people who do it already
academic philosophers

01:33:04.370 --> 01:33:07.450
and are not reading the
politics of the time

01:33:07.450 --> 01:33:12.100
though coming out Marx
making him as a philosopher

01:33:12.100 --> 01:33:15.243
which was already going on in the 1880s.

01:33:16.090 --> 01:33:18.280
Rather weirdly, Engels of course,

01:33:18.280 --> 01:33:21.050
wanted to make him a
materialist philosopher

01:33:21.050 --> 01:33:22.200
and therefore onboard

01:33:22.200 --> 01:33:25.520
with the physical science of that time.

01:33:25.520 --> 01:33:30.060
And a Hegelian systematizing philosopher

01:33:30.060 --> 01:33:33.090
to keep him in the German mainstream

01:33:33.090 --> 01:33:36.540
doing both of these at the same time

01:33:36.540 --> 01:33:39.850
in his works of the 1880s

01:33:39.850 --> 01:33:42.960
when he revisits the 1840s

01:33:42.960 --> 01:33:45.930
and this of course not
really very accurate

01:33:45.930 --> 01:33:49.420
40 years on in his recollections.

01:33:49.420 --> 01:33:53.023
So this is a complicated story
of overlapping receptions.

01:33:54.090 --> 01:33:59.090
One can make Marx into a Hegelian.

01:33:59.830 --> 01:34:03.660
I think it's really part of
the politics of the 1920s

01:34:03.660 --> 01:34:07.110
where people are trying to read Marx

01:34:07.110 --> 01:34:10.540
outside of the Orthodox Marxist

01:34:10.540 --> 01:34:15.320
materialist framework
of deterministic laws.

01:34:15.320 --> 01:34:18.640
They're picking up on
Hegel whom they assume

01:34:18.640 --> 01:34:21.830
can only be read in an idealist way.

01:34:21.830 --> 01:34:24.960
They don't quite want to say
that Marx is an idealist.

01:34:24.960 --> 01:34:26.660
He's just a Hegelian.

01:34:26.660 --> 01:34:29.900
They've pick up on the
young Hegelian discourse

01:34:30.830 --> 01:34:34.940
which they can find in the Marx archive,

01:34:34.940 --> 01:34:36.660
and then do some research on

01:34:37.520 --> 01:34:40.480
which had been leftover and ignored.

01:34:40.480 --> 01:34:42.280
Engels doesn't really revive it.

01:34:42.280 --> 01:34:44.270
And actually nobody revives it

01:34:44.270 --> 01:34:47.420
because everybody is onboard
with materialist science

01:34:47.420 --> 01:34:48.527
until the 1920s

01:34:48.527 --> 01:34:51.450
and we get this Hegel revival

01:34:51.450 --> 01:34:55.450
both in Weimar, Germany and in France.

01:34:55.450 --> 01:34:58.310
So some of the most influential

01:34:58.310 --> 01:35:00.940
French Hegelian reception of Marx

01:35:02.100 --> 01:35:05.460
comes in lectures actually
given at the Sorbonne

01:35:06.780 --> 01:35:09.100
So I give them some credits in that

01:35:09.100 --> 01:35:12.050
they're reading Marx
outside the materialist

01:35:12.050 --> 01:35:15.440
Orthodox deterministic framework.

01:35:15.440 --> 01:35:18.190
On the other hand, I think they mistake

01:35:18.190 --> 01:35:21.740
where those works and texts
are actually coming from.

01:35:21.740 --> 01:35:23.460
And it kind of depoliticized it

01:35:23.460 --> 01:35:25.340
as it makes what I think

01:35:25.340 --> 01:35:27.883
is actually an exciting
political intervention.

01:35:31.000 --> 01:35:34.200
- We have four questions in the chat.

01:35:34.200 --> 01:35:37.800
I think I'm going to count the forth

01:35:37.800 --> 01:35:40.913
from Marco Lata as pretty well answered.

01:35:42.463 --> 01:35:46.720
He or they ask whether
dialectics and critique

01:35:46.720 --> 01:35:48.630
were terms in common use

01:35:48.630 --> 01:35:50.630
given what you've said about Hegel

01:35:50.630 --> 01:35:52.620
and the milieu in which Marx was writing.

01:35:52.620 --> 01:35:55.300
I think it's pretty clear that they were

01:35:55.300 --> 01:35:57.920
but Marco if you have more
specific questions about that,

01:35:57.920 --> 01:36:00.880
you can certainly write
Professor Carver or myself.

01:36:00.880 --> 01:36:03.530
Then let's go to Isaac Kirk-Davidoff

01:36:03.530 --> 01:36:05.223
if he's still in the room.

01:36:07.760 --> 01:36:08.593
- [Isaac] Yeah.

01:36:08.593 --> 01:36:09.800
Well, hi.

01:36:09.800 --> 01:36:12.090
Professor Carver, I
just wanted to know that

01:36:12.090 --> 01:36:14.180
like there's so much imagery in "Capital."

01:36:14.180 --> 01:36:16.000
There's like tables, dancing,

01:36:16.000 --> 01:36:18.050
there's vampires he references,

01:36:18.050 --> 01:36:19.980
like Robinson Crusoe,

01:36:19.980 --> 01:36:22.500
and what's my favorite
footnote of "Capital."

01:36:22.500 --> 01:36:25.743
I just wanna know what you
make of all the imagery,

01:36:27.178 --> 01:36:29.050
is this like reaching to describe things

01:36:29.050 --> 01:36:31.460
that like political economy
doesn't have a language for,

01:36:31.460 --> 01:36:33.490
that Marx doesn't have a language for?

01:36:33.490 --> 01:36:36.110
Or is it more like just
for the sake of the reader?

01:36:36.110 --> 01:36:39.093
Like what do you make of all this imagery?

01:36:39.980 --> 01:36:41.990
- Well, I think that's a
really interesting question

01:36:41.990 --> 01:36:46.990
and my story on this is
that in the early 1980s

01:36:47.440 --> 01:36:50.630
I gave a word by word
chapter by chapter class

01:36:50.630 --> 01:36:55.057
with someone else's
seminar class on capital

01:36:55.057 --> 01:36:57.083
and I gave it several years running.

01:36:58.331 --> 01:37:01.480
And so I've got a little bit daydreamy

01:37:02.490 --> 01:37:05.670
going through the book,

01:37:05.670 --> 01:37:07.810
chapter one for the Nth time.

01:37:07.810 --> 01:37:11.390
So I got out my little yellow highlighter

01:37:11.390 --> 01:37:14.030
and just started highlighting the images

01:37:14.030 --> 01:37:17.740
'cause I was interested
in metaphor at the time.

01:37:17.740 --> 01:37:20.620
And I began to realize that there's

01:37:20.620 --> 01:37:24.800
a whole world of occult imagery there

01:37:24.800 --> 01:37:28.560
some of which is biblical from Revelation

01:37:28.560 --> 01:37:32.270
and some of which just
arrives from the occults

01:37:32.270 --> 01:37:33.430
like the tables turning,

01:37:33.430 --> 01:37:36.920
there's famously the
vampires, there's werewolves.

01:37:36.920 --> 01:37:40.790
So, I've got up my
highlighter and I made a list.

01:37:40.790 --> 01:37:42.250
And I read a short article

01:37:42.250 --> 01:37:46.440
which was published in the then
Times Literary Supplements.

01:37:46.440 --> 01:37:50.400
And it's one of the chapters
in "The Postmodern Marx"

01:37:50.400 --> 01:37:51.973
which you might enjoy.

01:37:53.481 --> 01:37:57.310
And the further answer is I thought, well,

01:37:57.310 --> 01:37:58.530
what is all this doing though?

01:37:58.530 --> 01:38:00.870
That's not just to entertain us.

01:38:00.870 --> 01:38:03.650
There must be some kind of thread.

01:38:03.650 --> 01:38:06.263
So, I think the threads goes like this,

01:38:06.263 --> 01:38:10.500
it's a classic kind of
enlightenment argument

01:38:10.500 --> 01:38:13.930
against ignorance and superstition.

01:38:13.930 --> 01:38:18.930
And it's coded against religion in general

01:38:20.937 --> 01:38:25.020
and it does actually tie
into political economy.

01:38:25.020 --> 01:38:27.240
So the argument goes like this.

01:38:27.240 --> 01:38:32.240
Okay, enlightenment post-Hegelian
liberals of the time,

01:38:32.700 --> 01:38:35.080
those who've read David Friedrich Strauss

01:38:36.552 --> 01:38:37.940
on the life of Jesus

01:38:37.940 --> 01:38:41.120
and don't buy the literal truth

01:38:41.120 --> 01:38:43.920
of the Christian confessional anymore.

01:38:43.920 --> 01:38:46.210
We know you're all past that.

01:38:46.210 --> 01:38:51.010
Look, what I'm showing you is that

01:38:51.010 --> 01:38:53.980
the world of commodity production

01:38:53.980 --> 01:38:57.430
which we're looking at
through empirical testimony

01:38:57.430 --> 01:39:02.040
and through works of political economy

01:39:02.920 --> 01:39:05.487
is just as mystificatory

01:39:05.487 --> 01:39:09.450
and just as performative
as we might say these days.

01:39:09.450 --> 01:39:14.450
That is commodities are, as
Marx says, really strange.

01:39:14.870 --> 01:39:18.010
You just think there's something
in a shop with a price tag.

01:39:18.010 --> 01:39:22.190
But let's try to think
about them as really weird.

01:39:22.190 --> 01:39:25.330
How is it that they have a price on them?

01:39:25.330 --> 01:39:27.240
What does price mean?

01:39:27.240 --> 01:39:29.300
He says, "Price is often language

01:39:29.300 --> 01:39:32.740
that commodities speak to each other?"

01:39:32.740 --> 01:39:36.910
This is getting really
strange and stranger.

01:39:36.910 --> 01:39:39.680
How is it that we have markets

01:39:39.680 --> 01:39:43.800
with forces moving by themselves

01:39:43.800 --> 01:39:46.740
which we have created
that control our lives

01:39:46.740 --> 01:39:49.320
in which we bow down and worship.

01:39:49.320 --> 01:39:51.740
This is the fetishism of commodities.

01:39:51.740 --> 01:39:55.593
Well, fetishism at the
time wasn't (indistinct)

01:39:55.593 --> 01:40:00.593
It was the projection of human
qualities onto stone idols

01:40:02.830 --> 01:40:06.440
such that they command
the humans will seem to

01:40:06.440 --> 01:40:09.490
and the humans believe
that the stone idols

01:40:09.490 --> 01:40:11.640
have magical powers and money,

01:40:11.640 --> 01:40:14.123
and monetized behavior,

01:40:15.278 --> 01:40:19.010
and all the things that
neoliberals complain about,

01:40:19.010 --> 01:40:21.970
and all the boxes we have
to take on the tax forms.

01:40:21.970 --> 01:40:25.490
So just all instances of being

01:40:25.490 --> 01:40:28.583
trapped within this performative,

01:40:29.570 --> 01:40:34.570
ritualistic world of commodity concepts

01:40:35.790 --> 01:40:38.510
that we all believe in because we have to.

01:40:38.510 --> 01:40:40.320
But hey, look, historically,

01:40:40.320 --> 01:40:42.020
this was made up historically.

01:40:42.020 --> 01:40:43.800
There was a time when
we didn't have money,

01:40:43.800 --> 01:40:45.380
we didn't have commodities.

01:40:45.380 --> 01:40:46.730
We didn't have capital.

01:40:46.730 --> 01:40:49.950
We got on, we traded
things, we produced things.

01:40:49.950 --> 01:40:51.380
Let's not go back there

01:40:51.380 --> 01:40:52.870
but then let's realize that

01:40:52.870 --> 01:40:54.820
commodity, production, and capitalism

01:40:54.820 --> 01:40:57.730
don't have to be here forever.

01:40:57.730 --> 01:41:00.220
So there's a historical
argument going along now

01:41:00.220 --> 01:41:02.410
along with an intellectual argument,

01:41:02.410 --> 01:41:07.410
which is that, okay, woke up liberals,

01:41:07.440 --> 01:41:09.960
you don't believe in this
religious stuff anymore.

01:41:09.960 --> 01:41:13.920
Apply the same argument
to the commodity stuff.

01:41:13.920 --> 01:41:17.880
It's just as weird as
believing in the incarnation

01:41:17.880 --> 01:41:20.830
and the Holy Spirit and
the Immaculate Conception.

01:41:20.830 --> 01:41:24.130
It's just it's got dressed
up as political economy

01:41:24.130 --> 01:41:27.540
or economics and we don't
have to bow down and worship

01:41:27.540 --> 01:41:30.520
once we realize it's a collection of

01:41:30.520 --> 01:41:33.320
human concepts that we act out.

01:41:33.320 --> 01:41:36.430
So there is a substantive way

01:41:36.430 --> 01:41:38.730
that some people could have recognized

01:41:38.730 --> 01:41:40.373
and it's part of the satire.

01:41:41.280 --> 01:41:44.160
It's just not because it works both ways.

01:41:44.160 --> 01:41:46.090
I mean, it's outlandish to say

01:41:46.090 --> 01:41:47.620
that capitalists are werewolves

01:41:47.620 --> 01:41:49.620
and capitalism is a vampire

01:41:49.620 --> 01:41:51.410
sucking the blood of the workers.

01:41:51.410 --> 01:41:54.650
But there's loads of
empirical stuff put in there

01:41:54.650 --> 01:41:56.100
to show exactly that

01:41:56.100 --> 01:41:59.240
and Marx quotes horrifying stuff

01:41:59.240 --> 01:42:01.600
by the time you get to part eight

01:42:01.600 --> 01:42:04.433
about what happens in the
Indian so-called mutiny.

01:42:05.400 --> 01:42:07.970
And other things like that from the maths,

01:42:07.970 --> 01:42:09.810
from the writing sort of colonialists

01:42:09.810 --> 01:42:12.340
and anti-colonialists themselves.

01:42:12.340 --> 01:42:14.530
So the whole thing is very joined up,

01:42:14.530 --> 01:42:18.720
you just have to get all the
way through to part eight,

01:42:18.720 --> 01:42:22.713
to see how it works where
horror meets horror.

01:42:25.330 --> 01:42:26.340
- You have two more questions.

01:42:26.340 --> 01:42:27.900
I'm mindful of the time.

01:42:27.900 --> 01:42:31.380
People on the East Coast
want to get to dinner

01:42:31.380 --> 01:42:34.850
and people in the UK want to get to bed.

01:42:34.850 --> 01:42:38.670
So let me just give you a summary
of the last two questions.

01:42:38.670 --> 01:42:42.290
One is from Ali Ecta who's
a student in the seminar

01:42:42.290 --> 01:42:46.390
and this follows up on the
last things you were saying.

01:42:46.390 --> 01:42:48.820
What do you think about
the theological rhetoric

01:42:48.820 --> 01:42:51.410
in "Capital" that Marx occasionally uses

01:42:51.410 --> 01:42:54.337
like quoting Jesus, "They
know not what they do."

01:42:54.337 --> 01:42:58.017
"Is this a satire," he asks,

01:42:58.017 --> 01:43:03.017
"or is this putting himself
into a messianic position?"

01:43:04.140 --> 01:43:06.410
And the last question is

01:43:06.410 --> 01:43:10.430
going back to what you said
about "The Communist Manifesto."

01:43:10.430 --> 01:43:12.930
Isn't it unfair to characterize

01:43:12.930 --> 01:43:15.120
the demands of the manifesto as liberal

01:43:15.120 --> 01:43:16.960
given the politics of the time?

01:43:16.960 --> 01:43:19.950
It was opposed by liberals as you said.

01:43:19.950 --> 01:43:23.980
Wouldn't it be more likely to think of it,

01:43:23.980 --> 01:43:25.660
I guess he means as communist

01:43:25.660 --> 01:43:27.910
or at least supporting
the worker's struggle?

01:43:28.970 --> 01:43:32.450
- Okay, I think anything from...

01:43:33.430 --> 01:43:38.430
Anything about their purports
to come from religion

01:43:38.610 --> 01:43:40.850
that's said by Marx with a straight face

01:43:40.850 --> 01:43:42.803
is totally satirical.

01:43:43.980 --> 01:43:48.170
He arrived at atheism very early on

01:43:48.170 --> 01:43:51.193
and this put him on the
radical edge of everybody.

01:43:52.240 --> 01:43:55.394
You could be seriously persecuted.

01:43:55.394 --> 01:43:56.600
You couldn't get a job.

01:43:56.600 --> 01:44:00.020
You couldn't do anything, much of anything

01:44:00.020 --> 01:44:04.593
as an atheist if word
got around about you.

01:44:05.640 --> 01:44:07.930
So, that is all totally satirical.

01:44:07.930 --> 01:44:12.660
It belongs to the same thing as putting

01:44:14.280 --> 01:44:15.880
and this is a powerful argument.

01:44:15.880 --> 01:44:19.280
I mean the whole business
of economic production

01:44:19.280 --> 01:44:24.280
under capitalism in the
same box as religious faith

01:44:24.520 --> 01:44:26.900
seems to be a serious category mistake.

01:44:26.900 --> 01:44:28.810
We're used to keeping
them in separate boxes.

01:44:28.810 --> 01:44:30.800
Oh well, I go to the shops

01:44:30.800 --> 01:44:33.380
but then I have my faith elsewhere.

01:44:33.380 --> 01:44:34.380
Marx is saying, well look,

01:44:34.380 --> 01:44:37.943
it's the same kind of
socially made up phenomenon.

01:44:39.245 --> 01:44:41.870
And so, I mean Marx went the whole way

01:44:41.870 --> 01:44:43.790
with Feuerbach and Strauss

01:44:43.790 --> 01:44:47.780
which is that religion generally
and Christianity of course

01:44:47.780 --> 01:44:49.950
as part of that is a projection of

01:44:49.950 --> 01:44:52.060
human ideas, concepts, and values

01:44:52.060 --> 01:44:56.040
on to things that are either
stone idols or don't exist,

01:44:56.040 --> 01:45:00.463
or are truths that
supposedly come from books,

01:45:01.330 --> 01:45:03.890
all of which require human interpreters

01:45:03.890 --> 01:45:05.250
in authority structures.

01:45:05.250 --> 01:45:09.150
So, I mean, that's deeply satirical.

01:45:09.150 --> 01:45:14.150
What's interesting is how
learned he was on Christianity

01:45:15.150 --> 01:45:16.670
and Bible study.

01:45:16.670 --> 01:45:19.810
I mean, he really knew it off completely

01:45:19.810 --> 01:45:21.720
and he's completely expert with it.

01:45:21.720 --> 01:45:22.990
And it's great language

01:45:22.990 --> 01:45:24.690
and it's another reason why

01:45:24.690 --> 01:45:28.730
I think the Lutheran Bible is
really the key to his prayers.

01:45:30.040 --> 01:45:32.350
The other question about liberalism,

01:45:32.350 --> 01:45:35.620
I think we have to go back again
to the politics of the time

01:45:35.620 --> 01:45:37.100
when being liberal,

01:45:37.100 --> 01:45:40.040
the German-speaking state
wasn't being liberal,

01:45:40.040 --> 01:45:41.883
it was being treasonous.

01:45:43.850 --> 01:45:47.890
You could be put in jail for
advocating popular sovereignty,

01:45:47.890 --> 01:45:51.100
for advocating constitutionalism.

01:45:51.100 --> 01:45:52.550
It was horrendous

01:45:52.550 --> 01:45:57.550
and remember that the
French Revolution of 1830

01:45:57.810 --> 01:46:01.310
is easily living memory.

01:46:01.310 --> 01:46:04.490
I mean risk their own lifetimes.

01:46:04.490 --> 01:46:08.780
I mean the rulers East of
the Rhine are terrified

01:46:08.780 --> 01:46:10.360
that's going to happen to them.

01:46:10.360 --> 01:46:12.480
The mob are going to come and get them,

01:46:12.480 --> 01:46:15.010
demand more votes from more people.

01:46:15.010 --> 01:46:17.420
And rather unfortunately for them

01:46:17.420 --> 01:46:21.660
Louis-Philippe sort of ran off.

01:46:21.660 --> 01:46:23.220
Didn't defend his throne,

01:46:23.220 --> 01:46:26.550
he ran off to Twickenham in London

01:46:26.550 --> 01:46:29.620
and set up his kingdom in exile.

01:46:29.620 --> 01:46:31.920
And you've got to be
pals with Queen Victoria.

01:46:35.139 --> 01:46:37.310
They were really terrified of this.

01:46:37.310 --> 01:46:39.740
And so to come back with our idea

01:46:39.740 --> 01:46:41.590
that we're all in a system where

01:46:43.670 --> 01:46:46.410
liberalism is part of the mainstream,

01:46:46.410 --> 01:46:48.080
the opposite is communism,

01:46:48.080 --> 01:46:53.030
really mistakes, the politics
of the period quite badly.

01:46:53.030 --> 01:46:55.650
Those demands are revolutionary demands

01:46:55.650 --> 01:46:57.440
for constitutionalism.

01:46:57.440 --> 01:47:02.090
And those people who signed
those demands got put on trial

01:47:02.090 --> 01:47:05.200
and Marx argued his way
out of a jury conviction

01:47:05.200 --> 01:47:07.250
and got tried in absentia.

01:47:07.250 --> 01:47:09.740
And some of these people went to prison

01:47:09.740 --> 01:47:11.960
and they went into exile.

01:47:11.960 --> 01:47:14.940
And they didn't all go to nice places even

01:47:14.940 --> 01:47:18.333
those nicest Soho in London
which wasn't very nice.

01:47:21.260 --> 01:47:24.440
- This is a way to just pause

01:47:24.440 --> 01:47:28.203
a much longer discussion
about Marx and his radicality.

01:47:29.240 --> 01:47:32.810
You've given us a really
radical reading I think,

01:47:32.810 --> 01:47:34.130
and a lot to think about.

01:47:34.130 --> 01:47:38.370
I wanna thank you very much
for your time and your effort,

01:47:38.370 --> 01:47:40.020
and all your work over many years

01:47:40.020 --> 01:47:43.193
to be able to sum things
up so beautifully for us.

01:47:44.410 --> 01:47:45.243
- Thank you.

01:47:45.243 --> 01:47:46.460
It's been an absolute delight.

01:47:46.460 --> 01:47:49.763
I've enjoyed the questions
obviously very considerably.

01:47:50.740 --> 01:47:52.570
I'm tempted to go back to share a screen

01:47:52.570 --> 01:47:57.113
and show you the book with
the volcano on the front.

01:47:58.140 --> 01:48:01.667
That was actually my idea in that

01:48:02.610 --> 01:48:07.610
the publishers wanted nature on the cover.

01:48:07.740 --> 01:48:10.500
And I thought, well, nature,

01:48:10.500 --> 01:48:13.390
I looked at the covers
which were all gardens,

01:48:13.390 --> 01:48:16.320
which was I thought,
well, that isn't gonna do.

01:48:16.320 --> 01:48:20.260
What garden is Marx gonna evoke.

01:48:20.260 --> 01:48:23.440
So I thought, well, let's go for metaphor.

01:48:23.440 --> 01:48:25.190
So I thought about the great wave,

01:48:25.190 --> 01:48:27.420
a tidal wave, a tsunami,

01:48:27.420 --> 01:48:30.173
some sort of unstoppable force.

01:48:31.200 --> 01:48:33.820
And the designers came back

01:48:34.884 --> 01:48:36.770
and they said, "Well,
here's some Great Waves

01:48:36.770 --> 01:48:38.187
including Hiroshige,"

01:48:39.580 --> 01:48:40.930
who's out of copyright.

01:48:40.930 --> 01:48:42.950
And they said, "But it
does make it look like

01:48:42.950 --> 01:48:44.227
a surfing book."

01:48:45.270 --> 01:48:49.160
And they said, "Well,
how about volcanoes?"

01:48:49.160 --> 01:48:51.300
And I said, "Hey, that's a great idea."

01:48:51.300 --> 01:48:53.247
So they came up with a volcano

01:48:53.247 --> 01:48:55.070
and a few weeks later,

01:48:55.070 --> 01:48:57.477
I happened to be reading Marx's

01:48:57.477 --> 01:48:58.960
"Speech to The People's Paper

01:48:58.960 --> 01:49:01.190
which is a very early
work written in English

01:49:01.190 --> 01:49:03.908
I think not entirely by him.

01:49:03.908 --> 01:49:07.498
He actually talks about
the forthcoming year,

01:49:07.498 --> 01:49:10.803
you know, the proletariat
as a volcanic eruption.

01:49:13.100 --> 01:49:15.430
- We hope that-
- The book channel this Marx.

01:49:15.430 --> 01:49:16.580
I mean, that's obvious.

01:49:16.580 --> 01:49:18.030
Anyway, thank you very much.

01:49:18.030 --> 01:49:19.460
It's been delightful.
- Thank you, Terrell.

01:49:19.460 --> 01:49:22.450
Given the amount of people
who were in the audience,

01:49:22.450 --> 01:49:25.980
it seems like the magma is getting hot.

01:49:25.980 --> 01:49:28.110
I want to say to everyone before they go

01:49:29.090 --> 01:49:32.290
that this series so beautifully kicked off

01:49:32.290 --> 01:49:34.560
by Professor Carver is
gonna go on next week.

01:49:34.560 --> 01:49:39.110
Marcello Musto, Musto from York
University is gonna be here.

01:49:39.110 --> 01:49:41.100
We will have William Clare Roberts

01:49:41.100 --> 01:49:43.980
talking about the literary
dimensions of the text.

01:49:43.980 --> 01:49:47.870
We have people talking
about reproductive labor,

01:49:47.870 --> 01:49:49.603
about racial capital,

01:49:50.450 --> 01:49:52.220
about the economic questions.

01:49:52.220 --> 01:49:54.600
So I hope you tune back in

01:49:54.600 --> 01:49:56.020
for the rest of the series.

01:49:56.020 --> 01:49:57.610
Thanks very much.

01:49:57.610 --> 01:49:58.443
- Thank you.

